var tafsirData_9 = {
  "1": "This opening verse of Sūrah At-Tawbah comes without the usual basmalah, a deliberate omission that signals the gravity of what follows. The declaration is a complete dissolution of treaty obligations: “a discharge from all obligations” means that Allah and His Messenger are no longer bound by the pacts that had been made with those polytheists who repeatedly broke their word. The historical context is vital here. After the Treaty of Ḥudaybiyyah, several Arab tribes entered into alliances with the Muslims, but many of them—most notoriously the Quraysh and their confederates—violated the terms openly. They attacked allied tribes, conspired against the believers, and proved that their agreements were worthless. As the time for the pilgrimage approached in the ninth year after Hijrah, the Muslims needed to secure the sacred precincts and establish that the era of dealing treacherously with treaties was over. The verse addresses the believers who were party to those treaties, reminding them that the discharge comes directly from Allah and His Messenger, so it carries ultimate authority. When we break the wording into its parts, “a discharge from all obligations” is a sweeping cancellation, not a selective adjustment. The phrase “by Allah and His Messenger” leaves no room for negotiation; it is a divine and prophetic decree. The expression “to the polytheists you have entered into treaties with” identifies the recipients: those idolaters with whom the Muslims had formal agreements. This verse is not a sudden breach of faith by the Muslims; rather it is a public annulment of pacts that the other side had already rendered void through their own actions. The principle here is that treaties are sacred in Islam, but they cannot be a one‑way street. When the other party systematically destroys the basis of trust, the injured side has the right to declare the agreement null. This discharge was proclaimed on the greatest day of pilgrimage, making it a matter of public record heard by all of Arabia. It also serves as a prelude to the four‑month grace period mentioned in the next verse, during which those who wished to make amends or leave peacefully could do so. The underlying message is one of justice: continued peace with those who keep their word, and a clear severance from those who do not.",
  "2": "The four‑month period granted here is an extension of the discharge announced in the first verse, giving the polytheists time to reflect, relocate, or accept Islam without being attacked. “You may travel freely through the land” is a guarantee of safe passage, a remarkable provision in a context where warfare was the norm after treaty violations. This safe‑conduct allowed those who were hostile to move about unmolested and either return to their own territories or come to a decision about faith. The four months began, according to the most authoritative reports, on the day of the greater pilgrimage when the declaration was read aloud, the tenth of Dhul‑Ḥijjah, and ran until the tenth of Rabīʿ al‑Thānī. So it was not an arbitrary period; it was tied to the sacred months and the pilgrimage season, during which fighting was already prohibited by Arab custom and later by Islamic law. The second part of the verse introduces a sobering warning: “but know that you will have no escape from Allah.” No matter where they travelled or how they evaded the Muslim forces, they could not flee Allah’s grasp. This is a call to spiritual reckoning, reminding them that physical safety within those four months does not mean ultimate impunity. The final phrase, “Allah will disgrace the disbelievers,” announces that persistent rejection will end in humiliation, either in this world through defeat or in the Hereafter through punishment. The disgrace here is not arbitrary; it is the natural consequence of defying truth after it has been made clear. The offer of safe travel combined with a solemn warning reveals the Qur’anic approach: clarity, opportunity, and then accountability. It also dismantles any misconception that the subsequent command to fight was an ambush; the polytheists were given every chance to rectify their stance.",
  "3": "This verse details the public proclamation that took place on the day of the greater pilgrimage, the Day of Sacrifice during the Ḥajj. The Prophet, peace be upon him, first sent Abū Bakr to lead the pilgrimage and then dispatched ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib to read these verses aloud to the assembled crowds at Minā. The phrase “to all people” shows the universal scope of the announcement: it was directed not only at the treaty‑breakers but at every tribe present, ensuring that no one could later claim ignorance. The core message is that Allah and His Messenger are now “free of the polytheists,” meaning the obligations of protection and alliance are dissolved. Immediately after this severance, the verse opens the door to reform: “if you repent, it will be better for you.” This demonstrates that the purpose was never mere expulsion or destruction, but transformation. Repentance, sincere turning away from idolatry and towards the worship of the One God, would bring them all that is good. “But if you turn away” indicates stubborn refusal, and the warning follows that they will have no escape from Allah. Then comes the striking instruction: “give good news to the disbelievers of a painful punishment.” This is an ironic expression; the phrase “good news” is used for something terrifying to underscore that what awaits them is certain and fully deserved. It also emphasizes the Prophet’s role as a warner. The historical report that ʿAlī stood and recited, “No disbeliever will enter Paradise, and no polytheist may perform pilgrimage after this year, and no one shall circumambulate the House naked,” highlights how the declaration translated into concrete rulings that purified the sacred precincts and affirmed the exclusivity of tawḥīd.",
  "4": "Even amidst the sweeping annulment of treaties, Allah carves out a clear exception for those who remained faithful. The verse specifies two conditions: they honoured every term of their treaty, and they did not support any enemy against the believers. Those who met both criteria were to be treated with full justice; the believers were commanded to honour their treaty with them until its natural expiration. This refutes any claim that Islam promotes blanket hostility or aggression. The principle is that integrity is reciprocal. The command “honour your treaty with them” is a binding directive, not a recommendation. The verse then grounds this command in a quality beloved to Allah: taqwā, mindfulness of Him. “Surely Allah loves those who are mindful” means that keeping one’s word even with non‑Muslims when they have kept theirs is an act of piety that attracts divine love. This is consistent with the general Quranic insistence on fulfilling contracts and covenants. The historical background includes certain tribes that had pacts with the Muslims and never violated them, such as some clans of Khuzāʿah or Banū Ḍamrah. They were allowed to live in peace until their treaty periods ended. This verse also teaches believers that moral conduct is not conditional on the faith of the other party; justice and faithfulness are absolute values in Islam. It sets a standard that governs international relations: agreements must be observed, and only a proven breach by the other side justifies termination.",
  "5": "This verse is one of the most frequently misquoted passages when removed from its context. The opening, “But once the Sacred Months have passed,” refers directly to the four‑month period of safe passage given earlier. The polytheists intended here are specifically those who broke their treaties and were given that grace period. The commands that follow are military instructions: to engage them wherever they are found, to capture, besiege, and ambush them. These are tactical measures appropriate to a state of war against a treacherous enemy, not a licence for indiscriminate violence. The specific mention of “kill the polytheists” must be read with the restriction “who violated their treaties” understood from the flow of the verses. After the Sacred Months elapsed, those who had persisted in hostility were to be fought. Crucially, the verse immediately pivots to the door of mercy: “But if they repent, perform prayers, and pay alms-tax, then set them free.” The very people who were to be fought become untouchable the moment they embrace faith, establish the prayer, and give the zakāh. The three conditions are marks of genuine conversion and entry into the Muslim community. The phrase “set them free” means all military actions against them must cease. “Indeed, Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” explains why: divine forgiveness overwhelms past transgressions once repentance occurs. This verse, far from being a call to perpetual warfare, presents a clear path to peace. The Prophet, peace be upon him, applied this in practice: those who accepted Islam were left unharmed, and even those who did not but sought protection were granted safety, as the very next verse shows.",
  "6": "This verse provides an immediate balance to the preceding military command. Even during a state of conflict, if a single polytheist comes seeking protection—literally, “seeking your neighbourhood”—the Prophet is ordered to grant it. The purpose is profoundly merciful: “so they may hear the Word of Allah.” The safe conduct is not merely physical shelter; it is an opportunity for the person to listen to the Qur’an, to understand its message, to ask questions and witness Islam firsthand. The command then goes further: after they have heard, if they do not accept the faith, the believers must “escort them to a place of safety.” The individual must be delivered to a location where they are secure from harm, not mistreated or coerced. The reason given is “for they are a people who have no knowledge.” Their hostility and idolatry are rooted in ignorance, and exposure to the truth is the remedy. This principle shaped Islamic rules of engagement: a non‑combatant who seeks amān must be protected and given the chance to learn. The verse demonstrates the very heart of the Islamic mission—to convey the message with wisdom and to prioritise guidance over vengeance. It also shows that the goal of struggle is not the elimination of a particular people but the removal of the barriers between them and the truth.",
  "7": "The rhetorical question “How can such polytheists have a treaty” expresses astonishment that serial treaty‑breakers could expect their agreements to remain honoured indefinitely. The exception “except those you have made a treaty with at the Sacred Mosque” points to those who entered into a pact in the vicinity of the Masjid al‑Ḥarām, which includes the Treaty of Ḥudaybiyyah. Some of those tribes, like the Banū Khuzāʿah, kept their word. For them, the instruction is clear: “as long as they are true to you, be true to them.” The reciprocity is absolute. The verse does not demand naivety; it demands faithfulness matched with faithfulness. Again the verse concludes by linking this conduct to the love of Allah for the mindful. This teaches that fulfilling treaties is a direct expression of taqwā. It also conveys that the Muslim community is to be a model of integrity; when they give their word, they uphold it, even if the world around them does not. This sets the stage for the next verse which explains why the habitual breakers cannot be trusted.",
  "8": "This verse answers its own question by exposing the inner reality of the treaty‑breakers. The idea of entering into a new treaty with them is unthinkable because if they gained power, they would discard all considerations of kinship and pact. “No respect for kinship or treaty” means that neither blood ties nor formal agreements would restrain them from harming the believers. This is not conjecture; it was proven by their repeated betrayals, such as the Quraysh’s attack on the Banū Khuzāʿah, which directly violated the terms of Ḥudaybiyyah. The verse then notes that whatever pleasant words they speak are merely flattery: “They only flatter you with their tongues.” Outwardly they may profess good intentions to buy time, but “their hearts are in denial.” Their internal state is one of rejection and hatred. “Most of them are rebellious” highlights their deep‑seated defiance of moral norms. The verse gives believers a realistic understanding of the enemy they faced. It also warns against being deceived by sweet talk when actions prove otherwise. The lesson is timeless: evaluate agreements not by rhetoric but by consistent behaviour, and when a party shows by its track record that it respects neither family ties nor contracts, it cannot be trusted until it demonstrates genuine change.",
  "9": "Here the Qur’an summarizes the motivation behind the polytheists’ treachery. They exchanged the eternal truth for a “fleeting gain.” That gain could be worldly power, tribal pride, economic advantage, or social status. The phrase “over Allah’s revelations” shows that they were presented with clear signs, yet they turned away because of immediate worldly interests. Not only did they stray themselves, they actively “hindered others from His Way,” obstructing people from embracing Islam through propaganda, threats, and warfare. The exclamation “Evil indeed is what they have done!” is a divine judgment on their actions. It reinforces that the severance of ties was not because of personal animosity but because of their fundamental opposition to the truth and their effort to prevent its spread. This verse also serves as a warning to any who put worldly profit ahead of divine guidance and who use their influence to block others from faith.",
  "10": "This statement is both a repetition and a reinforcement of what was said earlier, but now it becomes a definition of their character. They do not observe any protective bond, whether of blood or covenant, with the believers. The phrase “It is they who are the transgressors” turns the tables on any claim that the Muslims were the aggressors. Transgression means crossing the boundaries set by God and by human decency. The polytheists’ habitual violation of pacts and family ties qualifies them as the true transgressors, fully deserving of the annulment of their treaties. This also prepares the ground for the next verse, which opens the door to repentance and brotherhood once again.",
  "11": "The contrast could not be sharper. The same people who were described as treacherous and rebellious can become “your brothers in faith” through three steps: sincere repentance from shirk and past wrongs, the establishment of regular prayer, and the giving of the alms‑tax. These three are not merely external acts; they indicate a complete internal transformation and entry into the Muslim community. The moment they do this, all previous enmity is erased, and they are granted the full rights of brotherhood. The phrase “your brothers in faith” is one of the warmest expressions in the Qur’an, conveying equality and affection. Then the verse adds that Allah makes His revelations clear “for people of knowledge,” meaning those who truly understand will appreciate the wisdom of this balanced approach—firmness against persistent treachery, but immediate embrace when genuine change occurs. It also implies that superficial readers might misinterpret the preceding verses because they lack the knowledge of context and the overarching Quranic principles of mercy and justice.",
  "12": "This verse addresses a further scenario: even after entering into a pact or accepting Islam, some may break their oaths and actively assault the religion by slandering it or attacking the community. In that case, the directive is to fight “the champions of disbelief,” the leaders and instigators who drive the hostility and who have a proven record of never keeping their word. The phrase “who never honour their oaths” identifies them as irredeemable in their current state. The purpose is again made clear: “so perhaps they will desist.” The fighting is not for annihilation but to bring an end to their aggression. This restrained and purposeful approach permeates the entire passage. It also shows that the Muslim community must be ready to defend itself against those who would destroy it from within or without, yet even then the goal remains the cessation of harm, not vengeance.",
  "13": "The rhetorical question aims to awaken the believers’ sense of justice and courage. The polytheists are described with three severe crimes: they broke their oaths, they plotted to expel the Messenger from Mecca, and they initiated the aggression. The conspiracy to expel the Prophet refers to the Quraysh’s plan to assassinate or banish him from his homeland, the very event that led to the Hijrah. “And attacked you first” reminds the believers that they were not the aggressors; the battles of Badr and Uḥud were fought because the Quraysh came out to destroy the Muslim community. The question “Do you fear them?” addresses the hesitation that might arise from worldly considerations—the enemy’s numbers, wealth, or reputation. It then redirects that fear: “Allah is more deserving of your fear.” If you are true believers, your primary concern should be Allah’s command and His displeasure, not the enemy’s might. This is a powerful call to prioritize divine authority over human intimidation. It connects directly to the next verse’s promise of divine support.",
  "14": "This is the divine command to engage in battle, accompanied by five promises. “Allah will punish them at your hands” means the believers become the instruments of Allah’s justice in this world. The punishment is the defeat they will suffer. “Put them to shame” adds public disgrace to their loss. “Help you overcome them” promises victory. “And soothe the hearts of the believers” addresses the emotional wounds inflicted by years of persecution, treachery, and loss. The believers had suffered greatly, and there is a recognition that seeing justice served brings healing. This soothes the grief and anger that had built up over time. The verse, therefore, is not glorifying violence for its own sake but explaining that the battle against the treaty‑breakers was a necessary step toward establishing peace, security, and emotional closure for the oppressed community.",
  "15": "The soothing mentioned in the previous verse is now specified as “removing rage from their hearts.” The deep‑seated anger and bitterness that the believers harboured against those who had betrayed and killed their loved ones would be removed by Allah through the victory He granted. This is both a psychological and spiritual healing. The phrase “And Allah pardons whoever He wills” injects a note of divine mercy even into the discussion of battle. Among those enemies, some would eventually repent and be forgiven, as indeed happened with many Quraysh leaders after the conquest of Mecca. Allah’s will in pardoning is based on His knowledge and wisdom, not on human expectation. The closing attributes, “All-Knowing, All-Wise,” assure that every decision—who is punished, who is forgiven—is made with perfect understanding and purpose. This prevents believers from feeling that they must take personal vengeance, because ultimate judgment belongs to Allah.",
  "16": "This verse shifts the focus inward to the Muslim community itself. The command to fight serves as a test. The believers should not assume that their mere profession of faith is sufficient; Allah will test them to distinguish the sincere from the insincere. “Proving who among you truly struggles in His cause” means that through the call to arms, the true mujāhidīn will be separated from those who hold back. Added to this is the test of loyalty: they must not take intimate allies other than Allah, His Messenger, and the believers. In the context of the upcoming campaign to Tabūk, this was a direct challenge to those who secretly sympathised with the hypocrites or the enemy. The verse ends by reminding that Allah is fully aware of all actions, so no one can hide their true intentions. This is a spiritual call to self‑examination before the external battle begins.",
  "17": "The polytheists used to boast of their service to the Sacred Mosque, providing water to pilgrims and caring for the Kaʿbah. This verse strips them of that claim. It is not fitting for them to maintain the mosques of Allah because their open disbelief cancels any merit their actions might have had. “Their deeds are void” means all their charitable and religious services are rendered worthless in the sight of Allah due to their association of partners with Him. The announcement that they will dwell in the Fire forever is a stark warning that no amount of worldly service can compensate for the absence of true faith. This verse laid the groundwork for the purification of the Sacred Mosque from idolatry, which was completed when Mecca was conquered and the idols were removed.",
  "18": "The true caretakers of the mosques are defined by five qualities: belief in Allah and the Last Day, establishment of prayer, payment of zakāh, and fearing none but Allah. The phrase “fear none but Allah” is particularly significant because it excludes those who serve the mosques for worldly prestige or out of fear of people. The maintenance of mosques includes both physical upkeep and spiritual leadership. The verse then says, “It is right to hope that they will be among the truly guided,” which means that those who possess these qualities can reasonably expect to be counted among the rightly guided. It is an encouragement for believers to take up the responsibility of building and serving places of worship with pure intentions. This also contrasts sharply with the previous verse’s condemnation, establishing that mosques are for the people of tawḥīd, not for those who associate partners with Allah.",
  "19": "This verse directly rebuts a specific boast that some Quraysh leaders made. Historical reports mention that al‑ʿAbbās or others prided themselves on their service to the pilgrims and the upkeep of the Sacred House, considering it equivalent to faith and jihad. The question challenges that assumption: could providing water and maintaining the Mosque possibly stand on the same level as believing in Allah and the Last Day and striving in His cause? The answer is emphatic: “They are not equal in Allah’s sight.” Acts of service without īmān are hollow and carry no weight. The verse then declares that Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people, meaning those who persist in this false equivalence and who reject true faith are left to their misguidance. The principle is clear: the value of any deed is rooted in the belief that motivates it. This also reassures the believers that their sacrifices of faith, emigration, and jihad far outweigh any worldly service the pagans might offer.",
  "20": "The verse sets the true hierarchy of honour. Three qualities are listed: belief, emigration for the sake of Allah, and striving with both wealth and lives. This specifically describes the earliest Companions, the Muhājirūn, who left their homes and possessions and fought in the path of Allah. “Greater in rank in the sight of Allah” is the real measure of superiority, not lineage, wealth, or tribal standing. The phrase “It is they who will triumph” is an absolute promise of success, whether in this world through victory or in the Hereafter through eternal reward. This verse serves as an uplifting conclusion to the section, motivating the believers to press forward and not be disheartened by the enemy’s claims or numbers. It also seamlessly leads into the next passage, where the reward of such believers is further detailed. For the listener, it reinforces the message that the sacrifices demanded by Allah are never in vain; they elevate a person’s rank and guarantee ultimate triumph.",
  "21": "After establishing in the previous verse that those who believe, emigrate, and strive with their wealth and lives are greater in rank and will triumph, this verse reveals the substance of that triumph. The good news comes directly from “their Lord,” a possessive that conveys intimacy and honour. The first element of the glad tidings is “His mercy,” a comprehensive divine compassion that envelops the believers and admits them into realms of favour they could never earn by deeds alone. The Prophet, peace be upon him, taught that no one enters Paradise by their actions except through Allah’s mercy. This mercy is the foundation of all that follows. Next is “pleasure,” which is riḍwān, the state of Allah being eternally pleased with them. This is the highest aspiration of the believers, for divine displeasure would render any blessing bitter. The pleasure of Allah is the crown of heavenly reward, mentioned in other verses as “the greatest triumph.” Finally, “Gardens with everlasting bliss” describes the physical abode of delight. The word “everlasting” emphasises that the bliss is uninterrupted, never fading, never ending. The gardens are not merely orchards but realms of perpetual joy, with whatever the soul desires. The sequence moves from the unseen spiritual gift of mercy, to the relational gift of divine pleasure, to the tangible gift of Paradise. This good news was particularly important for the Companions who had left behind their homes, families, and wealth, because it assured them that what awaited them was incomparably better. It also serves to motivate those who might hesitate when called to struggle, reminding them that the promise of their Lord is true and generous. For all believers, this verse paints a picture of the ultimate success that makes any worldly sacrifice insignificant.",
  "22": "The phrase “to stay there for ever and ever” reinforces the permanence of the bliss mentioned before. Human experience in this world is defined by transience; every joy eventually ends. But the dwellers of Paradise will never be expelled, will never die, and will never experience decline. The repetition “for ever and ever” in the translation captures the Arabic emphasis on absolute eternity. This permanence is a crucial aspect of the reward because the fear of loss diminishes enjoyment. Knowing that the Gardens are an everlasting home eliminates any anxiety about the future. The verse then concludes with a general statement: “Surely with Allah is a great reward.” This directs attention away from the specific descriptions and towards the Giver. The reward is not merely gardens and pleasures; it is fundamentally something that lies with Allah, in His presence. The adjective “great” encompasses everything that human language cannot fully capture. This sentence also serves as a seal on the section, reminding the believers that the storehouses of divine generosity are limitless. It implicitly contrasts the fleeting gains the hypocrites and polytheists cling to with the immense, lasting reward reserved for the faithful. The placement of this reassurance before the stern instructions that follow about family ties and the call to battle is significant: it anchors the believer’s heart in what truly matters, so that the difficult commands become easier to bear.",
  "23": "With the magnificence of the reward established, Allah now addresses a deeply sensitive area of loyalty. The call “O believers” prepares the hearts for a command that cuts across the strongest natural bonds. Parents and siblings represent the closest circle of blood relation, and the term “trusted allies” translates awliyā’, implying intimate friendship, protection, and deep loyalty. The condition “if they choose disbelief over belief” is critical: it is not a blanket command to sever family ties, but a prohibition against making those who actively prefer kufr over īmān one’s closest confidants and protectors, particularly when such alliances could compromise faith or the community’s security. During the early period in Medina, some Muslims were torn between their love for their disbelieving relatives and the demands of their new faith, and some even passed sensitive information to them. This verse draws a clear boundary. To prioritise allegiance to disbelieving kin over the bonds of faith is described as “the true wrongdoers.” The word ẓālim here is profound: it indicates a person who puts something in the wrong place, the ultimate injustice being to place the rights of creation above the rights of the Creator. The verse does not cancel the obligation of kind treatment towards parents—as emphasised elsewhere—but it subordinates that relationship to the supreme loyalty owed to Allah, His Messenger, and the believing community. This was a necessary realignment of identity for the nascent Muslim society, ensuring that the community’s cohesion was not undermined by divided hearts.",
  "24": "This verse expands the list of potential competing loves. The Prophet is instructed to put forward a comprehensive inventory of everything a person naturally holds dear: parents, children, siblings, spouses, the wider clan, accumulated wealth, business ventures whose downturn one fears, and comfortable dwellings. Each of these represents a legitimate attachment, but they become a test when they outweigh the love for Allah, His Messenger, and jihad in His path. The phrase “more beloved to you” shifts the discussion from mere outward alliance to the heart’s deepest affection. The command to “wait until Allah brings about His Will” carries a severe warning: those who persist in loving these worldly attachments more than the divine cause should expect a painful consequence when Allah’s decree unfolds. It might be defeat, punishment, or simply being left behind from the ranks of the truly faithful. “Allah does not guide the rebellious people” identifies them as fāsiqūn, those who step outside the bounds of obedience. This verse was a powerful address to the Muslims who hesitated to join the Tabūk expedition because it was harvest time, the weather was hot, and the journey was long. It reminded them that all the things they feared to leave behind were from Allah in the first place, and that no worldly benefit compares to what lies with Him. The verse calls for an honest self‑examination of one’s priorities, a lesson that remains urgently relevant.",
  "25": "To reinforce the lesson that reliance must be on Allah, not on material advantages, the verse recalls a recent historical event. The Muslims had experienced numerous victories, but the Battle of Ḥunain served as a humbling corrective. Ḥunain took place after the conquest of Mecca, when the Muslims numbered twelve thousand. Some of them felt a sense of self‑confidence, saying, “We will not be defeated today because of small numbers.” The verse captures this: “when you took pride in your great numbers.” But that multitude “proved of no advantage.” The enemy, the Hawāzin and Thaqīf tribes, launched a surprise attack in a narrow valley, and the Muslim army was thrown into confusion. The earth, vast as it was, “seemed to close in on you,” a psychological description of the terror and claustrophobia of the ambush. The result was that many turned back in retreat, leaving the Prophet with only a small group of steadfast Companions. This vivid recollection is not to shame the believers but to teach them a fundamental principle: numbers and worldly strength do not guarantee success. Victory is from Allah alone. This served as a direct parallel to the call for Tabūk, where some might have been overconfident or, conversely, daunted by the enemy’s might. By recalling Ḥunain, Allah reminds them that the same power that turned their defeat into victory then can do so again, provided they place their trust in Him rather than in their resources.",
  "26": "After the chaos described in the previous verse, the tide turned through direct divine intervention. “His reassurance” translates sakīnah, a profound inner calm and tranquillity that descends from Allah. This sakīnah settled first upon the Messenger, who remained steadfast, and then spread to the believers around him. It replaced the panic with firmness and courage. The sending down of “forces you could not see” refers to the angels who fought alongside the believers, a reality mentioned elsewhere and confirmed by the Prophet, peace be upon him. Their presence was invisible to the human eye but tangible in its effects. The disbelievers were then punished, many being killed or captured, and their camp was plundered. The concluding phrase, “Such was the reward of the disbelievers,” ties the event to a universal law: those who oppose Allah’s Messenger and His cause will eventually face defeat and punishment. The verse is deeply reassuring to the believers facing the daunting campaign to Tabūk, because it demonstrates that when human means fail, Allah’s aid arrives in ways beyond expectation. It also underscores the importance of internal state—when the heart is filled with divine serenity, external circumstances can be overcome.",
  "27": "Even after the punishment of the disbelievers at Ḥunain, the door of repentance remained open. “Allah will turn in grace” refers to tawbah, the acceptance of repentance. Many of the defeated Hawāzin embraced Islam after the battle, and the Prophet, peace be upon him, treated them generously, returning captives and granting gifts. The phrase “whoever He wills” indicates that divine mercy is not arbitrary but is guided by His wisdom; those who genuinely turn to Allah are the ones He wills to forgive. The two concluding names, All-Forgiving and Most Merciful, are perfectly placed after the recounting of both punishment and grace. They remind the believers that Allah’s mercy can overtake even those who have been enemies. For the Muslims preparing for Tabūk, this served as a hope that their own shortcomings could be forgiven and that even the enemy might one day become brothers. It also prevented the believers from developing a triumphalist vengeance mentality, because the ultimate aim is that people come to faith and receive mercy.",
  "28": "This verse establishes a permanent ruling for the sanctity of the Sacred Mosque. The spiritual impurity of the polytheists is not physical dirt but the ritual and doctrinal impurity stemming from their idolatry. Because of this, they were forbidden from approaching the Masjid al‑Ḥarām after that year—the ninth year of Hijrah. This meant the end of the pagan pilgrimage and their naked circumambulation of the Kaʿbah. The prohibition was essential to purify the House of Allah for the worship of the One God alone. However, the Quraysh and other tribes’ pilgrimages had brought considerable trade and economic benefit to Mecca. Anticipating the worry that this ban might cause financial hardship, the verse immediately addresses the believers’ potential fear of poverty: “If you fear poverty, Allah will enrich you out of His bounty, if He wills.” This is a direct challenge to rely on Allah’s provision rather than on the revenues generated by idolatrous practices. The enrichment promised came true; the Islamic pilgrimage brought far greater numbers and prosperity, and the spoils of subsequent battles enriched the community. The verse closes with two names that underscore the perfection of this decree: All-Knowing, because He knows the future economic implications, and All-Wise, because the command serves a profound spiritual purpose. The verse thus combines ritual purity with trust in divine provision.",
  "29": "This verse extends the call to engage in combat, but now it is directed towards a specific category: the People of the Scripture who meet three conditions. They do not truly believe in Allah and the Last Day according to the monotheistic creed, they do not forbid what Allah and His Messenger have prohibited, and they do not adopt Islam as their religion. The command to fight them is until they pay the jizyah, a tax levied on non‑Muslims living under Islamic rule. The phrase “willingly submitting” translates ʿan yadin, which has been interpreted as paying it by hand in a state of submission and not through an intermediary, symbolising their acceptance of Muslim governance. “Fully humbled” translates ṣāghirūn, meaning they acknowledge their status as subjects under the protection of the Islamic state. This verse was the legal basis for the jizyah system. It is crucial to note that this fighting is not aimed at forced conversion; the jizyah allows them to retain their religion while living under the security and justice of Muslim rule. The campaign of Tabūk was directed against the Byzantine Empire and its Arab allies, who were largely Christians. This verse clarified the objectives: hostilities would cease if they embraced Islam or agreed to pay the jizyah. This provides a framework for a pluralistic society where non‑Muslims are protected in exchange for this tax, which exempts them from military service and zakāh. The verse underscores that the ultimate goal is either their entry into the truth or their peaceful integration under a just order.",
  "30": "The verse exposes the theological distortions that led these communities away from pure monotheism. It reports that some Jews claimed Ezra was the son of God—a belief not mainstream today but attested in certain ancient Jewish sects. The Christians are cited for their doctrine of the divine sonship of the Messiah. Both assertions are labelled “baseless assertions,” mere words unsupported by truth or revelation. The phrase “only parroting the words of earlier disbelievers” indicates that this is not an original deviation but an imitation of the pagans who also assigned sons to God. The exclamation “May Allah condemn them!” is a powerful expression of divine displeasure. The closing rhetorical question, “How can they be deluded from the truth?” expresses astonishment that they could be so far misled. The verse aims to highlight the gravity of such claims and to prepare the believers to see the battle not as mere political expansion but as a struggle to uphold tawḥīd against entrenched falsehood. It also serves as a warning to Muslims not to fall into exaggerated veneration of righteous figures.",
  "31": "This verse clarifies a profound form of idolatry that crept into the previous scriptures’ communities. Taking rabbis and monks as lords does not mean they formally prayed to them, but that they gave them the authority to declare what is lawful and unlawful, obeying them even when it contradicted God’s commands. An authentic hadith relates that when this verse was revealed, ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, a former Christian, said to the Prophet, “We did not worship them.” The Prophet replied, “Did they not make lawful what Allah had made unlawful and you followed them, and make unlawful what Allah had made lawful and you followed them?” ʿAdī acknowledged this, and the Prophet said, “That is worship of them.” Alongside this, the Christians’ deification of the Messiah is mentioned. The verse then reaffirms the core of all divine messages: they were commanded to worship only One God. The kalimah, “There is no god worthy of worship except Him,” is proclaimed, followed by the declaration that He is far above the partners they ascribe. This directly ties the combat against the Byzantines and their allies to the restoration of pure worship. It also reminds Muslims that blind obedience to scholars in matters that contravene clear revelation can become a subtle form of shirk, a warning that remains exceptionally relevant.",
  "32": "The imagery of light and extinguishing it is powerful. Allah’s light refers to His religion, His revelation, and the guidance brought by the Prophet. The phrase “with their mouths” suggests that their weapons are words—slander, argument, blasphemy, conspiracy. They blow at the light as if to put it out, but their breath is feeble against a divine blaze. The verse emphatically declares that Allah will perfect His light, meaning Islam will spread, its message will be completed, and its truth will be made manifest. “Even to the dismay of the disbelievers” indicates that the opposition’s hatred will not prevent the divine plan. This verse gave immense confidence to the Companions as they faced the superpower of the time, the Byzantine Empire. For the modern believer, it is a reassurance that the truth of Islam is not dependent on human approval and that the efforts to suppress it will ultimately fail. The light here is not merely intellectual but spiritual, penetrating hearts and societies.",
  "33": "The previous verse’s promise of the perfection of light is now explained. The means by which Allah perfects His light is the sending of the Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth. “True guidance” is the Qur’an and the Sunnah, which lead to every good. “The religion of truth” is Islam, the complete way of life that conforms to reality and justice. The divine purpose is to make this religion prevail over all religions—meaning it will be dominant, its proofs superior, and its adherents honoured. This prevalence was fulfilled in the Arabian Peninsula during the Prophet’s lifetime and later spread far beyond. “Even to the dismay of the polytheists” reiterates that the enemies’ displeasure is irrelevant; the divine promise stands. This verse is a declaration that Islam is not one option among many but the final, complete revelation destined for supremacy. For those struggling in the path of Allah, it is a vision that defines the ultimate goal: the global establishment of truth, not for the pride of a nation but so that Allah’s guidance can reach all humanity.",
  "34": "After exposing the theological deviations, the sūrah turns to the financial corruption of religious leaders. Many rabbis and monks—the same class that was wrongly taken as lords—devour people’s wealth through bribery, selling indulgences, or unjust judgments. They also hinder others from the path of Allah by keeping them away from Islam. The verse then broadens to include all who hoard gold and silver and refuse to spend in Allah’s cause. Hoarding (kanz) is the accumulation of wealth without fulfilling its due obligations, particularly zakāh. The command to “give good news of a painful torment” is, as earlier, a severe irony. This verse was a direct critique of the wealthy who withheld support from the Tabūk expedition and of the hypocrites who encouraged that attitude. It establishes the principle that wealth is a trust and must circulate for the good of the community. The hoarding of precious metals is specifically mentioned because they were the primary forms of saved wealth. This remains a timeless warning that amassing riches while neglecting the rights of Allah and the needy invites a dreadful punishment.",
  "35": "The torment described is terrifyingly graphic. On the Day of Resurrection, the very gold and silver they hoarded will be heated in Hellfire until it becomes blazing hot, and then it will be pressed against their foreheads, sides, and backs. The specific mention of these three body parts may correspond to the ways they turned away from obligation: the forehead representing arrogant refusal, the sides their turning away, and the back their retreat from spending. A hadith in Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim describes a similar punishment for those who withhold zakāh from camels and cattle, confirming that this is not metaphorical but real. The words spoken to them, “This is the treasure you hoarded for yourselves. Now taste what you hoarded!” are a direct echo of their own greed. The treasure they thought they were preserving for their benefit has become the instrument of their torture. This verse, along with the previous one, is a powerful motivation to give generously in Allah’s cause, especially when the community is in need, as during the Tabūk campaign. It shifts the perspective from short‑term financial loss to eternal consequence.",
  "36": "This verse anchors the Islamic calendar in the divine order. The twelve lunar months are decreed from the very creation of the universe, a celestial law that the pre‑Islamic Arabs knew but manipulated. Among these twelve, four are sacred: Rajab, Dhul‑Qaʿdah, Dhul‑Ḥijjah, and Muḥarram. During these months, fighting was forbidden, a tradition that Islam largely upheld except in cases of self‑defence. “That is the Right Way” refers to the correct religion and the adherence to the divinely fixed calendar. The command “do not wrong one another during these months” emphasises that the sanctity must be observed, and transgressions in them are even graver. However, the verse immediately adds a practical reality: the polytheists, while sometimes respecting the sacred months, fought the Muslims collectively and often violated them. So the believers are instructed to “together fight the polytheists as they fight against you together,” meaning that in the face of a united enemy that disregards sacred limits, the Muslims may also engage in collective action even during these months if necessary. The verse closes by reminding that Allah’s aid is with those who maintain taqwā, ensuring that any military action remains bound by righteousness.",
  "37": "The practice of nasī’ was the pre‑Islamic manipulation of the calendar. The Arabs, addicted to raiding and warfare, found it inconvenient to observe a three‑month truce, so they would shift the sacredness from one month to another, postponing, for example, Muḥarram’s sanctity to Ṣafar to allow fighting. The verse calls this “an increase in disbelief” because it added the sin of altering divine law to the sin of idolatry. They were led far astray by it because they thought they were cleverly managing their affairs, but in reality they were compounding their rebellion. The phrase “only to maintain the number of months sanctified by Allah” highlights their superficial adherence to the form while destroying the substance. They kept the count of four sacred months but violated the specific months that Allah had designated. “Their evil deeds have been made appealing to them” explains the psychology of sin: persistent transgression blinds a person until they see their evil as good. The verse declares that Allah does not guide such people, meaning they are left to their own delusion. This condemnation of calendar tampering served to re‑establish the pure lunar calendar for the Muslim community and underscored the principle that divine commands must be followed exactly as prescribed, not modified for convenience.",
  "38": "The address now returns directly to the hesitant believers during the call to Tabūk. The tone is one of gentle rebuke and awakening. “What is the matter with you” expresses disappointment at their sluggishness. To “march forth in the cause of Allah” meant joining the expedition against the Byzantine forces. Their response was to “cling firmly to the land,” meaning they were deeply attached to their homes, farms, and comforts. The rhetorical question “Do you prefer the life of this world over the Hereafter?” cuts to the heart of the issue. It presents a binary choice: temporary worldly ease or eternal reward. The verse then provides the rational basis for the correct choice: “The enjoyment of this worldly life is insignificant compared to that of the Hereafter.” Whatever they feared losing—shade, water, family, business—is utterly trivial when weighed against the everlasting bliss of Paradise. This comparison was meant to shift their perspective and loosen the grip of worldly attachments. It speaks powerfully to all believers who find it difficult to sacrifice for their faith, reminding them that every worldly comfort is fleeting, while what lies with Allah is immeasurable.",
  "39": "The warning intensifies dramatically. Refusal to march forth will bring a painful punishment, whether in this world through humiliation and defeat, or in the Hereafter through the Fire, or both. This punishment is a direct consequence of disobedience. More starkly, Allah threatens to replace them with other people—a community that will be more obedient, more willing to sacrifice. This replacement happened historically to some extent; the early followers who showed reluctance were later supplanted in leadership by those who were more steadfast, and the message spread through others. The phrase “You are not harming Him in the least” is a profound statement of Allah’s self‑sufficiency. Human obedience or disobedience does not affect Him; it only affects the people themselves. This demolishes any arrogance or sense that Allah needs our worship. It is purely for our own benefit. The verse closes with “Allah is Most Capable of everything,” reminding them that the threat is not empty; He can easily bring about the change He warns of. This served to shake the believers out of their complacency and impel them towards action.",
  "40": "This verse delivers the ultimate rebuttal to any hesitation to support the Prophet. It begins by stating that the believers’ failure to help would not diminish him, because Allah has already proven He can support him alone. The reference is to the Hijrah, the most vulnerable moment of the Prophet’s mission. When the disbelievers drove him out of Mecca, he was only one of two—just him and Abū Bakr. In the cave of Thawr, with the pursuers outside, the Prophet reassured his companion with the immortal words, “Do not worry; Allah is certainly with us.” This is a lesson in absolute trust. Allah responded by sending down His sakīnah upon the Prophet, a divine calm that stilled the heart. Unseen forces—angels—were sent, and the outcome was that the disbelievers’ word was made lowest, utterly defeated, while the Word of Allah, the kalimah of tawḥīd, remained supreme. The two names Almighty and All-Wise seal the narrative: Almighty because no power can oppose Him, and All-Wise because every event, including the ordeal in the cave, was part of a perfect plan. For the believers facing Tabūk, this was a reminder that the same God who protected His Messenger in that desperate situation would grant victory again, regardless of the enemy’s apparent strength. It also honours Abū Bakr’s companionship, which earned him the title “the second of two.” The verse teaches that Allah’s support is not dependent on numbers; it is rooted in faith and reliance upon Him.",
  "41": "After reminding the believers of Allah’s support for His Prophet in the most desperate moment—alone in the cave—this verse issues a comprehensive call to action. The address “O believers” draws the community back into a collective responsibility. The command to “march forth whether it is easy or difficult” removes every possible excuse. Ease and difficulty cover all circumstances: youth and old age, wealth and poverty, hot weather and cold, personal comfort and hardship. The expedition of Tabūk was particularly difficult; it was the height of summer, the distance was great, the enemy was the superpower of the age, and the harvest was ripe. Yet Allah commands them to go regardless. The phrase “strive with your wealth and your lives” pairs financial sacrifice with physical sacrifice. In the battle of Tabūk, the wealthy Companions like ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān gave enormous sums, while others gave their physical effort and risked their lives. The verse declares that this striving is “best for you,” a compact statement that reorients their entire calculus. The benefit is not for Allah but for their own souls, in this world through honour and victory, and in the Hereafter through immense reward. The closing words “if only you knew” are a gentle lament, indicating that human perception is often blinded by immediate comfort, unable to grasp the vastness of divine recompense. This verse is a timeless call to the believers to remain in a state of readiness to sacrifice for their faith, refusing to let comfort become an obstacle to obedience.",
  "42": "This verse exposes the true nature of the hypocrites who stayed behind. The “gain” refers to immediate worldly booty, like that of a nearby raid. If the campaign had been close and promised quick material reward, they would have eagerly participated. But the journey to Tabūk was long, the spoils uncertain, and the danger high, so “the distance seemed too long for them.” Their reluctance was not based on genuine inability but on a cost‑benefit analysis of worldly profit. The verse predicts their behaviour after the believers return: they will swear by Allah, insisting that they truly wanted to go but lacked the means. Their oaths are elaborate but empty. The divine judgment is stark: “They are ruining themselves.” Their lies and desertion do not harm Allah or the Prophet; they destroy their own souls. The final phrase, “And Allah knows that they are surely lying,” strips away all pretense. No human court might convict them, but the Knower of the unseen has already pronounced the verdict. This teaches that sincerity cannot be faked before Allah, and that outward excuses will not avail when the heart is full of worldly calculation. It also warns believers against judging by appearances, because some people may swear eloquently while their hearts are far from the truth.",
  "43": "This gentle rebuke of the Prophet, peace be upon him, reveals his immense mercy and the high standard to which he was held. He had allowed some of the hypocrites to remain behind when they came with excuses, not wanting to be harsh. The verse opens with “May Allah pardon you,” indicating that his decision, though motivated by compassion, was not the ideal course of action at that moment. The question “Why did you give them permission before those who told the truth were distinguished from those who were lying?” points to the wisdom of waiting. Had he not excused them, the genuine believers who had valid reasons would have been forced to reveal themselves honestly, and the liars would have been exposed by their persistence in making false excuses. By granting permission early, the lines between the sincere and the hypocritical were blurred. This was a learning moment for the Prophet and, through him, for all leaders: leniency is good, but it should not come at the cost of clarifying the truth and exposing falsehood in critical matters of communal obligation. The rebuke also demonstrates the Qur’an’s honesty in recording even a corrective addressed to the Messenger, confirming that he is a human who is guided, and that ultimate perfection belongs only to Allah. For the community, it is a lesson that leadership requires discernment and sometimes firmness to protect the collective integrity.",
  "44": "This verse sets the benchmark for genuine faith. True belief in Allah and the Last Day produces a natural willingness to sacrifice. The ones who truly hold these beliefs “do not ask for exemption” from jihad with their wealth and lives. Their hearts are so convinced of the reward and the importance of the struggle that they would not seek to be excused except under the most dire and obvious necessity, and even then they would come with heavy hearts, not with excuses. The verse distinguishes between the faithful, who feel the weight of obligation, and the hypocrites, who eagerly seek loopholes. The second sentence, “And Allah has perfect knowledge of those who are mindful of Him,” serves as a reassurance that Allah knows the inward state of every person. Even if a believer’s situation genuinely prevents him from going, his sincere intention and taqwā are fully known to Allah, and he will be rewarded accordingly. The verse thus encourages believers to cultivate an attitude of readiness and to leave the judgment of hearts to Allah, while making it clear that a pattern of seeking exemption without valid cause is a sign of weak faith.",
  "45": "This verse draws the logical conclusion from the previous one. The only people who would seek to be excused from the struggle are those who lack genuine faith. They “have no faith in Allah or the Last Day” in a true, operative sense; whatever they claim with their tongues is contradicted by their actions. Additionally, their “hearts are in doubt.” They are not serene disbelievers but are “torn by their doubts,” wavering between belief and disbelief, never finding peace. This state of uncertainty makes every sacrifice seem too costly, because they are not convinced of the eternal reward. The verse masterfully diagnoses the psychological root of reluctance: a heart unsettled by doubt cannot commit to a costly course of action. For the sincere believer, this serves as a warning to guard against doubts and to seek certainty through knowledge and reflection. It also helps the community understand that the problem with the hypocrites was not merely laziness but a fundamental spiritual sickness. The cure is not harsher commands but a renewal of īmān that removes the hesitation caused by doubt.",
  "46": "This verse adds another layer of exposure. The hypocrites’ lack of intention is proved by their lack of preparation. A person who genuinely wants to travel for a campaign gathers provisions, arranges a mount, and gets ready. They did none of this, betraying their insincerity. Then the verse reveals a deeper divine decree: “But Allah disliked that they should go.” Because of their evil intentions, Allah did not want their presence among the sincere believers. Their going would not have added strength; it would have brought corruption. So “He let them lag behind,” creating circumstances—such as their own laziness and cowardice—that resulted in their staying. The passive phrase “it was said to them” may refer to the inner voice of their own nafs or the whisper of Shayṭān, or it could be the decree of Allah that they are consigned to remain with the helpless—the women, children, sick, and elderly who legitimately stayed. In any case, they were grouped with those who could not fight, which was a humiliation for able‑bodied men. This teaches that sometimes being prevented from a good deed is itself a punishment for hidden evil. The outward failure to act is a reflection of a prior inward failure.",
  "47": "The wisdom behind their being held back is now explained. If the hypocrites had joined the Tabūk expedition, they would have added no military value, only trouble. They would have “scrambled around,” moving through the ranks, whispering doubts, spreading rumours about the difficulty of the journey, the strength of the enemy, and the foolishness of the campaign. Their goal was to spread discord and break the unity of the believers. The verse acknowledges a vulnerability within the Muslim camp: “some of you would have eagerly listened to them.” Not all believers are equally strong; there are those with weak faith who can be swayed by persuasive talk. The hypocrites knew how to target such individuals. By keeping them away, Allah protected the army from internal collapse. The verse concludes that Allah has perfect knowledge of the wrongdoers; He knows their methods, their targets, and the harm they could cause. For the believers, this is a reassurance that Allah’s management of affairs, even when it appears as a setback, is rooted in deep wisdom. It also warns the community to be vigilant about internal subversion, then and now.",
  "48": "The current plotting is not new. The verse recalls their past attempts at causing discord. Earlier in Medina, the hypocrites had tried to undermine the Prophet’s authority, spread false rumours about his wives, and incite division between the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār. They “devised every possible plot,” scheming in every way their malice could invent. But all their efforts failed because “the truth came and Allah’s Will prevailed.” The truth refers to the clear proofs of Islam and the divine support given to the Messenger. No matter how intricate their schemes, they could not stop the unfolding of Allah’s plan. The result was “much to their dismay,” because the very thing they hated flourished. This historical reminder strengthens the Prophet and the believers, showing that the enemy’s conspiracies are ultimately futile. It also prepares them for the next round of hypocritical excuses and complaints. The lesson is that falsehood can plot extensively, but when Allah wills a matter, no human scheme can overturn it.",
  "49": "Another specific excuse of the hypocrites is recorded here. One of them, said to be al‑Jadd ibn Qays, came to the Prophet and asked for exemption from the Tabūk expedition, arguing that if he saw the beautiful Byzantine women, he would be tempted and fall into sin. So he presented himself as a man who feared temptation and wanted to protect his faith. The divine response cuts through the pretence: “They have already fallen into temptation.” By staying behind with insincere intention, by seeking to avoid a sacred duty, and by making false excuses, they are already deep in fitnah. Their supposed fear of sin is a mask for their cowardice and disobedience. The verse then declares that Hell will surely engulf the disbelievers, a sharp verdict that connects their action to the root cause—disbelief. This teaches that one cannot justify disobedience by claiming to avoid a lesser temptation. The greater temptation is abandoning Allah’s command. It also warns against the subtlety of the nafs, which can dress up desertion as piety. The sincere believer checks his motives and prioritises obedience over rationalisations.",
  "50": "The emotional state of the hypocrites regarding the Prophet’s mission is laid bare. When victory or booty comes to the Muslims, the hypocrites are grieved. They hate to see Islam flourish because it threatens their status and exposes their insincerity. In contrast, when a hardship or defeat occurs—such as the initial confusion at Ḥunain or any temporary setback—they feel a perverse joy. They then say, “We took our precaution in advance,” meaning that their decision to stay behind was wise and saved them from suffering. They “turn away, rejoicing,” utterly detached from the pain of the believers. This exposes their complete alienation from the community. They are not neutral; they are emotionally invested in the failure of the believers. For the Prophet and the sincere, this is a painful revelation, but it also equips them to understand that the hypocrites’ words and actions are driven by this deep‑seated enmity. It is a lesson that a true believer’s heart beats with the community; their joy is our joy, and their suffering is our suffering. The hypocrite’s heart is the opposite.",
  "51": "In the face of the hypocrites’ gloating over disasters, the Prophet is instructed to teach the believers a formula of unshakeable faith. The phrase “Nothing will ever befall us except what Allah has destined for us” affirms absolute divine decree. Whether victory or defeat, ease or hardship, everything is within the knowledge and will of Allah. This does not lead to passive fatalism but to courageous action, because the believer knows that his ultimate safety is not in human planning but in Allah’s protection. “He is our Protector” means Allah is the Mawlā, the one who takes charge of the believers’ affairs and secures their ultimate good. The response to the hypocrites’ smugness is not anger but a deepening of trust. The concluding instruction, “So in Allah let the believers put their trust,” makes tawakkul the defining characteristic of the community. This verse was a powerful source of strength for the Companions on the difficult Tabūk campaign, and it remains a refuge for believers in all times of hardship. It silences the inner whisper that says disaster means abandonment and replaces it with the conviction that everything is from the Wise, the Merciful.",
  "52": "The Prophet is instructed to contrast the two outlooks with striking clarity. The believers, in their march, expect one of only two outcomes, both of which are the best: victory, which brings honour and the spread of the faith, or martyrdom, which leads directly to Paradise. For a believer, both are a triumph. This reframes the entire concept of loss; there is no scenario in which a sincere warrior for Allah’s cause ultimately loses. The hypocrites, however, can only await torment. That torment may come “from Him,” through a natural disaster, a lightning bolt, or a sudden illness, or “at our hands,” through the believers being commanded to fight them or through the execution of justice. This is not a prayer for their destruction but a statement of fact about the consequences of their hypocrisy. The challenge “So keep waiting! We too are waiting with you” is a firm declaration of confidence. It leaves the matter in Allah’s hands and refuses to be intimidated by the hypocrites’ schemes. For the believers today, this verse offers a complete reframing of life’s trials: a believer’s path always leads to good, and the enemies of truth have nothing to wait for but ruin, unless they repent.",
  "53": "This verse addresses the hypocrites’ potential attempt to give charity in order to prove their loyalty or to silence criticism. The Prophet is told to declare bluntly that their donations will never be accepted. The reason is not the amount or the outward act but the state of the givers: “for you have been a rebellious people.” The word fāsiqūn implies persistent, wilful disobedience and departure from faith. An act of worship is only accepted when it springs from īmān and sincerity. The hypocrites lack both. This verse was a devastating blow to any attempt by them to pretend to be part of the community through financial contributions. It also establishes the crucial principle that Allah looks at hearts and intentions, not merely at the external deed. A charity given to earn a reputation, or to cover up hypocrisy, is worthless in the divine scales. This teaching safeguards the community from being impressed by the wealth of the insincere and reminds every Muslim that the purification of the soul is a prerequisite for any good deed to be accepted.",
  "54": "The previous verse’s verdict is now explained in detail. Three reasons are given for the rejection of their charity. First, they have lost faith in Allah and His Messenger; their kufr cancels all deeds. Second, their prayer, which is the pillar of religion, is performed only “half-heartedly,” with laziness, without true devotion, and often to be seen by others. Third, their spending is always accompanied by resentment; they give not out of love for Allah but grudgingly, feeling it as a loss rather than an investment for the Hereafter. These three points paint a picture of a person whose entire religious life is a hollow shell. They do the motions but the soul is absent. The verse teaches that outward acts without a believing and willing heart are rejected. It also serves as a mirror for believers to examine their own prayers and charity: do we give cheerfully? Do we pray with presence of heart? The hypocrisy condemned here can have degrees, and the remedy is to constantly renew one’s faith and intention.",
  "55": "The hypocrites often possessed considerable wealth and many children, which in the societal view were signs of success and divine favour. Allah warns the Prophet not to be impressed by these outward trappings. What appears as a blessing is actually an instrument of punishment for them. Their wealth brings them anxiety, greed, and fear of loss. Their children may be a source of worry and grief. Moreover, their attachment to these things keeps them from faith, so that when death comes, they depart from this world still disbelievers, having squandered their test. This is a profound redefinition of blessing and punishment. A comfortable life that leads to eternal loss is the worst form of torment. The verse redirects the believers’ gaze from material comparisons to the ultimate outcome. It teaches that we should never envy the prosperity of those who are distant from Allah, because that prosperity might be their ruin. True blessing is that which brings one closer to Allah and leads to a good end.",
  "56": "The hypocrites constantly swore oaths to convince the Muslims that they were truly part of the believing community. They would say, “We are with you, we believe as you believe.” But the verse declares, “they are not.” Their inner state is completely detached. The only motivation for their pretense is fear—fear of being treated as enemies, fear of losing protection, fear of being exposed and killed. Their Islam is a political cloak. This verse explains the phenomenon of religious affiliation based purely on social or political convenience. It was a warning to the Muslim community not to take every outward profession at face value, especially when the person’s actions and the company they keep indicate otherwise. At the same time, it directs the Prophet to leave their inner judgment to Allah, while dealing with them based on their outward claims, unless clear evidence of treachery emerges.",
  "57": "This vivid image reveals the inner distress of the hypocrites. They feel trapped in their double life, constantly afraid of exposure. If they could locate any secure shelter—a fortress, a mountain cave, an underground passage—that would allow them to escape the Muslim community and its demands, they would bolt towards it without a moment’s hesitation. Their presence among the believers is not a matter of choice but of painful necessity. This shows that the hypocrites were not comfortable fellow‑travellers; they were prisoners of their own lies. For the believers, this was a consolation: the very people who caused them trouble were themselves suffering a kind of torment, and their outward association was not a sign of genuine belonging. The verse also teaches that living a lie is a heavy burden; the hypocrite can never experience the peace of a single, honest life. The believer, in contrast, finds rest in the unity of his inner and outer self.",
  "58": "Another trait of the hypocrites surfaces: their attitude towards the distribution of charity and public funds. The Prophet was in charge of distributing zakāh and spoils. Some hypocrites, and also some new Muslims with weak faith, were critical of how he allocated these funds. If they received a share, they were pleased and considered it fair. If they were not given anything, they became enraged and accused the Prophet of injustice. Their standard was purely self‑interest, not principle. This behaviour reveals a transactional relationship with the faith: they support the leader only as long as they personally benefit. The verse exposes this ugly trait and serves as a warning to all believers to examine their own motives when they receive or do not receive worldly benefits from the community. The sincere believer trusts the leader’s judgment and is content, knowing that the ultimate reward is with Allah.",
  "59": "This verse presents the beautiful alternative to the ugly greed of the previous verse. Instead of complaining, the believers should be content with whatever they receive, recognizing it as a provision from Allah. The words they should say are an expression of profound trust: “Allah is sufficient for us!” This is hasbunā Allāh, a declaration of complete reliance. They affirm that Allah will give them from His bounty in the future, and so will His Messenger through just distribution. And their ultimate desire is directed to Allah alone, “To Allah alone we turn with hope,” indicating that their ambition is not for a few coins but for His pleasure and reward. This is a lesson in the etiquette of receiving: gratitude, trust in the provider, and redirecting one’s highest hopes towards the Hereafter. The verse teaches that inner contentment is a priceless treasure that frees a person from the tyranny of greed and the bitterness of unmet expectations.",
  "60": "After addressing the hypocrites’ criticism of alms distribution, Allah lays down the definitive categories for zakāh expenditure. This is the only verse in the Qur’an that details these categories, and it thus forms the foundation of the Islamic economic system of obligatory charity. The verse lists eight categories. First, the poor (fuqarā’) and the needy (masākīn), who are those without sufficient means. Then, those employed to administer the zakāh, meaning the collectors and distributors, who may be paid for their work. The fourth category is “those whose hearts are attracted to the faith,” new Muslims or influential people whose inclination towards Islam can be strengthened through financial support. Fifth, for freeing slaves, which includes ransoming captives and assisting in emancipation. Sixth, those in debt who cannot repay. Seventh, “for Allah’s cause,” which scholars have understood primarily as jihad and all works that serve the religion’s public good. Finally, the needy traveller stranded away from home. The verse concludes with “an obligation from Allah,” stressing that this is a divine decree, not a matter of human discretion. The two names All-Knowing and All-Wise assure that the allocation is perfectly designed to meet the needs of society. For the believers, this verse transforms zakāh from a vague charity into a structured system of social justice, ensuring that wealth circulates to those who need it most.",
  "61": "This verse exposes another form of verbal attack that the hypocrites used against the Prophet, peace be upon him. The phrase “He listens to anyone” was a sarcastic remark. They meant that the Prophet was gullible, that he would believe whatever anyone told him without verification, and that his ear was open to every rumour and excuse. It was a way of belittling his judgment and suggesting that he could be easily manipulated. The divine response instructs the Prophet to clarify the truth. “He listens to what is best for you” is a profound correction. The Prophet did listen attentively, but only to that which is good, truthful, and beneficial for the community. His listening was not a weakness but a mercy. He heard people’s problems, their confessions, their excuses, and he responded with wisdom. The verse then adds three qualities that define his character. First, “He believes in Allah,” meaning his trust and faith are in his Lord, not in gossip. Second, he “has faith in the believers,” meaning he trusts the sincere among them and treats them with goodwill. Third, “he is a mercy for those who believe among you.” This recalls the Qur’anic description of the Prophet as a mercy to the worlds. His compassionate listening, his patience with people’s stories, and his refusal to rush to judgment were all manifestations of that mercy. The verse ends with a stern warning: those who hurt the Messenger with such words will face a painful punishment. The crime of hurting the Prophet is not merely a personal offence; it is an attack on the conduit of revelation and leadership. This verse also teaches the believers the etiquette of speaking about the Prophet and the seriousness of even casual disparagement. It calls for deep respect and love for the one through whom they received guidance.",
  "62": "The hypocrites were masters of the strategic oath. They swore by Allah to the believers, attempting to convince them of their loyalty and innocence after they had made offensive remarks or stayed behind from battle. Their goal, as the verse states, was “to please you.” They sought social acceptance, the avoidance of conflict, and the material benefits of being seen as part of the community. The verse redirects their focus and exposes their misplaced priorities. The pleasure they should have been seeking was that of Allah and His Messenger. True believers orient their entire lives around earning divine and prophetic approval; human approval is secondary. The clause “if they are true believers” implies that their actions prove they are not genuine. A sincere heart cannot prefer the pleasure of people over the pleasure of Allah. This verse serves as a sharp self‑assessment tool for every Muslim. Are we performing religious acts, saying the right words, or making certain commitments primarily to please those around us—family, peers, society—or to please Allah? The hypocrites’ oaths were technically true in wording but false in intent, and the Qur’an exposes that hypocrisy cannot be papered over with solemn words. The lesson is that the direction of the heart in all actions must be towards Allah and His Messenger, not towards the shifting approval of human beings.",
  "63": "This verse presents a rhetorical question meant to shake the conscience of the hypocrites. Did they truly not understand the consequence of their opposition? The phrase “whoever opposes Allah and His Messenger” encompasses all forms of hostility, whether it is verbal mockery, secret plotting, or open defiance. The punishment is eternal Hellfire. The Qur’an describes it as “the ultimate disgrace,” a comprehensive shame that dwarfs any worldly honour they might have sought through their scheming. The word “disgrace” is particularly pointed because the hypocrites’ whole strategy was to avoid disgrace in this world by pretending to be believers. Yet they were earning the greatest possible disgrace in the Hereafter. This verse demonstrates that a short‑sighted focus on worldly reputation while neglecting eternal consequences is the height of foolishness. It also serves as a warning to all believers that opposition to divine authority, even in subtle forms, is a path to ruin. The verse encourages a healthy fear of Allah’s punishment, which is a necessary component of faith that keeps a person from moral compromise.",
  "64": "The hypocrites lived in constant dread of exposure. They knew that the Qur’an was being revealed gradually and that it often addressed the hidden secrets of people. They feared that a sūrah would come down and lay bare what was in their hearts—their mockery, their disbelief, their plots. This verse captures that anxiety and turns it into a challenge. The Prophet is told to say, “Keep mocking!” This is not a permission but a threat. Continue with your scornful talk, because Allah will certainly expose it. The phrase “bring to light what you fear” is a definite promise. Shortly after, verses were indeed revealed that unmasked their words and deeds, such as the incident where some of them joked, “We have not seen anyone like our reciters—more greedy, more lying, and more cowardly,” referring to the Prophet and the believers. The Qur’an recorded their words and answered them. So their worst fear came true. This teaches that no secret is hidden from Allah, and that the truth will eventually surface. It is also a comfort to the sincere believers who might be distressed by the whispered mockery of the insincere; they are assured that Allah is aware and will deal with it.",
  "65": "The verse now relates the actual incident. When confronted about their sarcastic remarks, the hypocrites immediately tried to downplay their words. They claimed it was just idle talk, mere joking to pass the time on the journey. They used this as a defence to avoid being held accountable. The response demolishes their excuse. The question “Was it Allah, His revelations, and His Messenger that you ridiculed?” leaves no room for ambiguity. The subject of their jesting was not a mundane matter; it was the most sacred realities. Mocking Allah, the Qur’an, or the Prophet is not an act of casual levity; it is a sign of deeply rooted kufr. The verse teaches that words are not weightless. Speech about religious matters carries immense responsibility. In a famous hadith, a man said something he thought insignificant, but it caused him to be thrown into Hell for seventy years. The hypocrites’ attempt to trivialise their blasphemy is a timeless tactic: when people are caught in offensive speech about the faith, they often say, “I was only joking,” or “You’re too sensitive.” This verse sets the standard that certain subjects are never to be treated with mockery, and that doing so indicates a heart that has not truly internalised belief.",
  "66": "The divine verdict is delivered. “Make no excuses” means that their justifications are utterly rejected. The judgment has been passed: they have committed kufr after having outwardly professed īmān. This is a declaration of apostasy. The phrase “lost faith after your belief” indicates that they had once entered the fold of Islam, at least outwardly, but their mockery and hypocrisy expelled them from it. The verse then mentions a division among them: some might be pardoned—meaning those who sincerely repented and amended their ways—while others would be punished for their persistence in wrongdoing. This is a consistent Qur’anic pattern: the door of repentance remains open even for the gravest sins, but those who refuse it face certain punishment. The verse also shows that collective guilt is not applied blindly; individuals are judged by their own actions. This was a warning to the hypocrites of Medina that their time for excuses was over, and they needed genuine repentance or they would face the consequences.",
  "67": "The Qur’an now paints a comprehensive portrait of the hypocritical character, applying to both men and women. They are all from the same cloth, sharing these traits. First, they encourage evil (munkar) and forbid good (maʿrūf). This is the exact opposite of the believers’ command. Where the believers call to virtue, the hypocrites promote vice and discourage righteousness. Second, they withhold what is in their hands, meaning they are miserly with their wealth, refusing to spend in charity or for the common good. Their greed is a hallmark. The verse then describes a terrifying spiritual law: “They neglected Allah, so He neglected them.” Because they ignored Allah’s remembrance, commands, and rights, Allah left them to their own devices, withdrawing His guidance and care. This is a form of divine punishment that comes through abandonment. The verse concludes that the hypocrites are indeed the fāsiqūn, those who have strayed far from obedience. This description serves as a mirror for believers to examine themselves: do we subtly encourage evil through our silence or actions? Do we close our hands when the community needs help? The neglect of Allah is the root cause, and it leads to a downward spiral.",
  "68": "The fate of the hypocrites is now stated without ambiguity. They are grouped together with the open disbelievers, receiving the same promise of eternal Hell. The phrase “it is sufficient for them” indicates that Hell is ample as a punishment and as an abode; it requires no addition. “Allah has condemned them” means they are under divine curse, banished from mercy. The punishment is “never-ending,” underscoring the severity of their crime. Hypocrisy, which corrupts the community from within, is treated with the utmost seriousness because it destroys trust and faith in the hearts of the weak. For the believers, this verse is a warning and a reassurance: a warning not to let any trace of hypocrisy enter their hearts, and a reassurance that the hidden enemies who cause so much pain will not escape justice. The clarity of the threat is meant to bring the sincere back to Allah and to isolate the corrupt.",
  "69": "The hypocrites are now compared to earlier nations that were destroyed. The comparison highlights their false sense of security. The previous nations were stronger, richer, and had more children, yet they were annihilated when they persisted in disbelief. “They enjoyed their share” means they took their portion of worldly pleasures, and “you have enjoyed your share” indicates the hypocrites are doing the same. Both also engaged in idle talk—vain conversation, mockery of the prophets, and immersion in trivialities. The outcome for those earlier peoples was that their deeds became void, worthless, and they were losers in both worlds. This serves as a direct warning: if the hypocrites follow the same path, they will meet the same end. History is full of lessons, but only those with insight learn. The verse teaches that material strength is no protection against Allah’s punishment. It also warns against the danger of being absorbed in the fleeting pleasures of this world and in empty talk, which distracts from the remembrance of Allah and the serious business of the soul.",
  "70": "The rhetorical question is a call to reflect on well‑known historical examples. Each mentioned nation had a messenger who brought clear proofs—miracles and divine guidance. The people of Noah were drowned, ʿĀd were destroyed by a fierce wind, Thamūd by a thunderous blast, the people of Abraham defied him and were defeated, the people of Midian (Shuʿayb’s people) suffered an earthquake, and the overturned cities of Lot were rained upon with stones and flipped. The verse emphasises that their destruction was not an act of divine injustice. “Allah would have never wronged them.” Rather, they wronged themselves by rejecting the truth and committing evils. This establishes that divine punishment is always a consequence of human choices. The hypocrites of Medina had heard these stories recited in the Qur’an, so they had no excuse. The verse calls every generation to study the past, not as mere tales but as warnings. The pattern is clear: when people mock the messengers and persist in rebellion, they seal their own fate. No one is too powerful to escape.",
  "71": "After the dark portrait of the hypocrites, the sūrah turns back to the beauty of the true believing community. The relationship among believers is described with the word awliyā’, translated as “guardians” or “allies.” This means they support, protect, and care for one another like a single body. This mutual guardianship is not based on blood or tribe but on faith. The verse then lists their qualities, directly contrasting them with the hypocrites. The hypocrites encouraged evil and forbade good; the believers encourage good and forbid evil. The hypocrites were miserly; the believers establish prayer and pay zakāh. The hypocrites neglected Allah; the believers obey Allah and His Messenger. This parallel structure makes the distinction crystal clear. The promise for such believers is that they will be enveloped in Allah’s mercy. “It is they who will be shown Allah’s mercy” indicates a special, continuous manifestation of divine compassion. The verse closes by reminding that Allah is Almighty, so He can reward them, and All-Wise, so His plan for the community is perfect. This verse lays the foundation for a caring, morally active society where faith translates into positive social action.",
  "72": "The reward for the believing men and women is now detailed in a manner that fills the heart with hope. The first promise is of Gardens with flowing rivers, symbolising unending delight and abundance. They will dwell there eternally, never to be expelled. Within those Gardens, they are promised “splendid homes,” pure and beautiful dwellings in the Gardens of Eternity, a place of settled peace. But then the verse elevates the reward to its peak: “and—above all—the pleasure of Allah.” The Arabic structure uses the word “wa riḍwānun mina Allāhi akbar” meaning the pleasure of Allah is greater than all that. This is the supreme gift, for the soul of the believer longs not just for comfort but for the knowledge that its Lord is pleased with it. The verse concludes, “That is truly the ultimate triumph.” All worldly triumphs—victory in battle, accumulation of wealth, fame—are shadows compared to this. For the Companions who had sacrificed everything for the cause, this verse was a balm and a motivation. It also makes clear that women are full partners in this spiritual journey and its reward, explicitly mentioned alongside men.",
  "73": "The command to the Prophet now combines the two fronts of opposition. Jihad against the disbelievers is a clear command, but the verse also commands struggle against the hypocrites. However, the form of this struggle differed. Against the disbelievers, it could be military. Against the hypocrites, it was through argument, exposure, legal measures, and a firm, uncompromising stance. The Prophet was never commanded to execute hypocrites for their hidden disbelief as long as they maintained outward peace; instead, he struggled against them by ignoring their excuses, withholding positions of trust, and not praying for their dead. “Be firm with them” means no leniency, no accepting their false justifications. The mention of Hell as their home reinforces the gravity of their state. This verse established a dual policy that protected the Muslim community from both external military threats and internal subversion.",
  "74": "This verse refers to a specific incident. During the return from Tabūk, a group of hypocrites plotted to assassinate the Prophet by pushing him off a mountain pass. They also made blasphemous remarks. When confronted, they swore by Allah they had said nothing. But Allah reveals the truth: they uttered a blasphemy, committed apostasy, and attempted a plot they failed to execute. The verse then expresses astonishment at their ingratitude: instead of being grateful for the wealth and prosperity that came through Islam, they repay Allah and His Messenger with resentment and treachery. “It is only through resentment that they pay Allah and His Messenger back for enriching them out of His bounty!” This is an indictment of the ugliest form of ingratitude, where blessing is answered with rebellion. The verse, however, leaves the door open: “If they repent, it will be better for them.” But if they persist, a painful torment awaits in this world—through exposure and defeat—and in the Hereafter, with no protector. This shows that even the gravest crimes can be forgiven with sincere repentance, but that stubborn refusal leads to complete ruin.",
  "75": "A new category of hypocrites is introduced. These were people who, in times of poverty, made solemn pledges to Allah. They promised that if Allah granted them wealth, they would become generous givers of charity and would join the ranks of the righteous. This vow was made in a moment of desire and perhaps sincerity. However, vows made to Allah are binding, and the heart’s true state is tested when the wealth actually arrives. This verse sets up the story that follows, teaching that a promise made in need can be easily forgotten in prosperity. It serves as a warning about the danger of making commitments to Allah that one is not fully prepared to keep. The verse also teaches that charity and righteousness are the expected responses to divine generosity, not hoarding and forgetfulness.",
  "76": "The test came and they failed. Allah granted them from His bounty; they became wealthy. Instead of fulfilling their vow, they withheld their wealth, refusing to give the charity they had promised. Worse, they “turned away indifferently,” meaning they abandoned the whole attitude of devotion and obedience that had led them to make the vow. Their hearts, now attached to their new riches, grew cold. This is a classic tale of how wealth can corrupt. The poverty that once made them turn to Allah was replaced by a self‑sufficient arrogance. This verse exposes the hypocrisy of conditional religiosity: a person claims they will be pious if only given certain means, but when the means arrive, they reveal their true attachment to dunyā. For the sincere believer, it is a call to fulfil one’s pledges and to recognise wealth as a test, not a reward in itself.",
  "77": "As a direct consequence of their broken vow and false promises, Allah punished them with a spiritual disease: hypocrisy was made to settle deeply in their hearts. It became a permanent stain, a persistent sickness that would remain with them until death, when they would meet their Lord and face the full reckoning. The verse explicitly states the cause: “for breaking their promise to Allah and for their lies.” This reveals a crucial psychological and spiritual law. Each unfulfilled vow, each lie told in the name of faith, hardens the heart and makes insincerity easier. Over time, the heart becomes so accustomed to falsehood that it can no longer distinguish truth from falsehood. The punishment is built into the sin. This is a terrifying warning to take one’s words, especially solemn oaths and promises to Allah, with the utmost seriousness. It also teaches that sincere repentance is urgent; delay allows the disease to become chronic.",
  "78": "The rhetorical question shatters any illusion of privacy. The hypocrites whispered their plots, made their secret vows, and concealed their mockery, thinking no one knew. The verse reminds them—and all humanity—that Allah knows their secret thoughts (sirr) and their most private conversations (najwā). He is the Knower of the unseen (ʿallām al‑ghuyūb). This is a fundamental attribute of Allah, and reflecting on it is meant to instil both fear and comfort: fear for those who hide evil, and comfort for the oppressed that their sighs and secret tears are fully known. For the believer, the awareness that Allah knows every hidden thought is the strongest motivation for sincerity. It is impossible to be a hypocrite if one truly internalises that Allah’s knowledge penetrates every layer of concealment.",
  "79": "This verse recounts another ugly incident. The Prophet called for charity for the Tabūk expedition. The wealthy Companions like ʿUthmān and ʿAbd al‑Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf gave enormous sums. The hypocrites accused them of showing off, saying they only gave to be seen. Meanwhile, poorer believers gave whatever small amount they could, such as a handful of dates or half a measure of grain. The hypocrites mocked them, saying, “Allah has no need of this paltry donation.” So the hypocrites slandered both the generous rich and the struggling poor. The response is severe: their mockery will be turned back on them. On the Day of Judgment, they will be the ones ridiculed and disgraced. The painful punishment awaits them. This verse protects the dignity of sincere donors, whether they give much or little. It condemns the cynical attitude that belittles people’s sacrifices. It also teaches that the value of charity is in its sincerity, not its amount, and that mocking a believer’s good deed is a very serious sin.",
  "80": "This verse was revealed concerning the leader of the hypocrites, ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy. When he was dying, his son, a sincere Muslim, asked the Prophet to pray for his forgiveness and to give his shirt for the burial shroud. The Prophet, out of his immense mercy, was inclined to do so. But this verse decisively forbade it. The number “seventy” is not a literal limit but an Arabic expression indicating a very large number; it means no amount of intercession will avail for them. The reason is that “they have lost faith in Allah and His Messenger.” Their persistent disbelief and hypocrisy have barred them from forgiveness. The verse closes by stating that Allah does not guide the rebellious people, those who have chosen to exit the bounds of faith. This was a crucial lesson for the Prophet and the community: divine mercy is immense, but it is not for those who die in a state of kufr and rebellion after the truth has been made clear. It also established the ruling that funeral prayers are not to be performed for known hypocrites. The verse highlights the balance between the Prophet’s compassionate nature and the divine limits that must be respected.",
  "81": "This verse captures the dark joy of the hypocrites who deliberately stayed behind from the Tabūk expedition. The phrase “rejoiced for doing so in defiance of the Messenger of Allah” shows that their staying was not a matter of necessity but an act of rebellion. They took pleasure in their disobedience, feeling they had outsmarted the Prophet and avoided a difficult journey. Moreover, they “hated the prospect of striving with their wealth and their lives in the cause of Allah.” This reveals that their hearts had no love for jihad or sacrifice; they saw it as a burden to be avoided. The historical context is the blistering summer heat of the Tabūk campaign. The hypocrites discouraged one another, saying, “Do not march forth in the heat.” It was a seductive argument because the physical discomfort was real. They used a worldly reality—the harsh weather—to justify abandoning a divine command. Allah instructs the Prophet to respond not by denying the heat but by redirecting their attention: “The Fire of Hell is far hotter!” The comparison is devastating. They were trying to escape temporary, physical heat by embracing an action that leads to eternal, unimaginable heat. The sentence “If only they could comprehend” expresses pity mixed with condemnation; their minds are so trapped by immediate comfort that they cannot foresee the ultimate consequence. For the sincere believers, this verse is a lesson in priorities: no worldly discomfort, no matter how intense, can compare to the punishment of the Hereafter. It also exposes the psychology of the hypocrites who seek to make disobedience attractive by magnifying present hardships. The reply given to them is a powerful rhetorical tool that believers can use internally whenever tempted to abandon a duty because of difficulty—the heat of Hell is far hotter than any struggle in this world.",
  "82": "This verse follows the hypocrites’ joy at staying behind with a chilling prophecy. The phrase “let them laugh a little” is a divine concession to their current state of false security. Their laughter is the laughter of those who think they have escaped hardship. It is a small, fleeting amusement tied to this world. The second half, “they will weep much,” shatters that illusion. The weeping is not a brief moment of regret but a prolonged, intense grief. This refers primarily to the punishment of the Hereafter, where the hypocrites will shed tears of anguish and remorse. It also manifested in this world through the exposure of their hypocrisy, their loss of trust, and the constant fear they lived under. The “little” and “much” are placed in sharp contrast to show the vast discrepancy between the temporary pleasure of sin and the eternal pain it brings. The phrase “as a reward for what they have committed” is deeply ironic. The word “reward” usually implies compensation for good deeds, but here it is used for the recompense of evil. Every act of mockery, every encouragement to abandon jihad, every moment of defiance is being stored up and will be returned to them in the form of endless sorrow. This verse serves as a powerful warning not to take delight in disobedience or in escaping religious obligations, because that laughter is short‑lived and will be replaced by tears. For believers, it is also a comfort: those who mock and avoid sacrifice may seem happy now, but their true state is one of impending grief.",
  "83": "The verse anticipates a future scenario. After the Tabūk expedition, the Prophet would return to Medina, and some of those hypocrites who had stayed behind might come forward and ask to join future military campaigns. Perhaps they saw the success and spoils, or they wanted to clear their names. However, Allah instructs the Prophet to refuse them permanently. The command is emphatic: they will never be allowed to march out with him or fight an enemy alongside him. The reason given is their previous choice: “You preferred to stay behind the first time.” This was a deliberate decision, not an inability, and it revealed their true character. The punishment is to be consigned permanently to the group of those who legitimately remain behind—the women, children, and sick. They are to be treated as if they are incapable, stripped of the honour of participating in jihad. This was a severe social and spiritual sanction. It demonstrates that repeated, unrepentant hypocrisy can lead to being excluded from the ranks of the believers in matters of trust and honour. For the community, it was a purification; the army would no longer be infiltrated by those whose hearts were not in the struggle. The verse also highlights the principle that opportunities for sacred service can be lost through one’s own choices. When a person repeatedly refuses a call to good, a time may come when the door is closed and the chance is gone. This should inspire believers to seize the moments of obedience when they are presented.",
  "84": "This verse establishes a definitive break with the hypocrites even in death. The Prophet is forbidden from performing the funeral prayer for any of them and from standing at their graveside. The funeral prayer is a profound act of intercession and a sign of the community’s bond with the deceased. To deny it is to declare that the bond of faith does not exist. The reason is explicitly stated: they “lost faith in Allah and His Messenger and died rebellious.” Their outward affiliation with Islam did not save them because their inner state was one of rejection. The term “rebellious” translates fāsiqūn, indicating they departed from the path of obedience. This verse was applied when ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy, the head of the hypocrites, died. His son, a sincere Muslim, asked the Prophet to pray for him and to give his shirt as a shroud. The Prophet initially inclined to do so, but this verse descended and he refrained. The prohibition serves multiple purposes: it demonstrates that faith is the only basis for spiritual solidarity; it removes any ambiguity about the status of known hypocrites; and it acts as a deterrent for those who might think they can live a life of hypocrisy and still receive the honours of a believer at death. For the community, it emphasises that loyalty is to faith, not to personalities or family ties. The verse also reminds every Muslim to be deeply concerned about the state in which they will die, for an apparently Muslim life that ends in hidden kufr is of no avail.",
  "85": "This is the second time in the sūrah that the Prophet is warned not to be impressed by the wealth and children of the hypocrites, the first being verse 55. The repetition underscores its importance. The hypocrites possessed considerable riches and many offspring, which in the eyes of society signalled success and divine favour. Allah warns that these are not signs of His pleasure; rather, they are instruments of torment. The torment occurs in this world through the anxiety, greed, and fear of loss that accompany wealth, and through the worry and grief that children can cause when they stray or when they are lost. Moreover, the attachment to these blessings prevents them from repenting and turning to Allah, so their souls depart from this world while they are still disbelievers. This is the ultimate disaster: to leave the world with the kalimah of tawḥīd absent from the heart. The verse dismantles the materialistic worldview that equates prosperity with divine approval. For believers, it is a reminder not to envy the worldly success of those who are distant from Allah, because that success is part of their trial and potential destruction. The truly blessed person is the one whose wealth and children bring them closer to Allah and help them attain a good end, not the one who simply possesses them in abundance.",
  "86": "This verse highlights the immediate reaction of the wealthy hypocrites to the revealed commands. When a sūrah would descend containing the call to faith and jihad, the wealthy among them would immediately seek exemption. They did not wait to understand the command or to assess their capacity; their first instinct was to avoid the obligation. Their request, “Leave us with those who remain behind,” shows that they wanted to be counted among the helpless—the women, children, and invalids—who were legitimately excused. This was a deliberate lowering of their status to escape duty. Their wealth, which should have made them foremost in contributing, became the very thing that made them cling to comfort and privilege. The verse exposes the correlation between material abundance and spiritual cowardice when that abundance is not grounded in faith. It also shows that the Qur’anic revelation was a continuous test, sifting the sincere from the insincere. For believers, this is a warning about the subtle danger of wealth: it can make a person seek comfort and exemption from the sacrifices that faith demands. It also teaches that one’s response to the call of Allah at the moment it is heard reveals the true condition of the heart.",
  "87": "The choice of the hypocrites is described as a preference. They actively chose to remain with those who were incapable of fighting. This was not a forced situation but a deliberate option. The phrase “with the helpless” further emphasises the shame of their position; able‑bodied, wealthy men grouping themselves with those who are physically unable. Then the verse reveals the spiritual cause behind this choice: “their hearts have been sealed.” The sealing of the heart is a consequence of persistent rejection and sin. When a person continually turns away from guidance, the heart gradually loses its ability to receive truth. The seal is not an arbitrary punishment but the natural result of their own actions. As a result, “they do not comprehend.” They cannot grasp the gravity of their situation, the importance of jihad, or the reality of the Hereafter. Their intellect functions in worldly matters, but regarding spiritual truth, they are blind. This is one of the most frightening descriptions in the Qur’an, and it calls every believer to guard their heart against hardness. The verse implies that if a person repeatedly chooses comfort over divine command, a time may come when they no longer even see their choice as wrong; they lose the capacity for sincere repentance.",
  "88": "The contrast with the previous verse is absolute. While the hypocrites stayed behind with the helpless, the Messenger and the sincere believers strove with both their wealth and their lives. This pairing of financial and physical sacrifice is the hallmark of true faith. Their striving was not reluctant but eager, and it covered every dimension of their being. The result is a comprehensive promise: “They will have all the best.” The Arabic expression al‑khayrāt means all good things—victory, honour, provision, and above all, the spiritual goodness of nearness to Allah. The verse then declares, “it is they who will be successful.” This is the true success, the f alāḥ, which is not measured by worldly gains but by achieving eternal salvation and the pleasure of Allah. The verse serves as an inspiring conclusion to the section, refocusing the believers’ attention on the right models. The Prophet and his Companions are the standards; their sacrifice is the path to all good. For every believer who feels the pull of comfort and the temptation to stay behind, this verse stands as a clear sign: real success belongs to those who strive.",
  "89": "The success promised in the previous verse is now detailed. The reward is not abstract; it is a prepared, ready destination. Allah Himself has prepared these Gardens, indicating the care and honour with which they have been made. “Gardens under which rivers flow” is the Qur’an’s most frequent description of Paradise, evoking an image of perpetual shade, water, and abundance. The eternality of the stay is emphasised with “to stay there forever,” removing any fear of ending or expulsion. Then the verse seals it with “That is the ultimate triumph.” The word “triumph” (fawz) conveys the idea of winning the greatest prize after a difficult contest. The struggle with wealth and lives was the contest, and the reward is this unimaginable victory. This verse gives the believers a vision that makes every sacrifice in the path of Allah light. It also stands in stark contrast to the fate of the hypocrites described earlier. For the Companions returning from Tabūk or preparing for future struggles, this was a continuous source of hope. For all believers, it reaffirms that the true measure of triumph is not a military victory or worldly gain, but entry into Allah’s eternal Gardens with His pleasure.",
  "90": "The sūrah now widens its scope to include the Bedouin tribes living around Medina. When the call to Tabūk was made, some of these nomads came to the Prophet with excuses, seeking permission to stay behind. Their excuses were varied, but they were a mix of genuine and false. The verse, however, focuses on a group that did not even bother to present an excuse: they simply remained behind, being “untrue to Allah and His Messenger.” Their absence was a direct act of betrayal, without even the pretence of justification. The verse warns that “the unfaithful among them will be afflicted with a painful punishment.” This includes those who lied in their excuses and those who stayed behind arrogantly. The mention of the Bedouins is significant because they were often new to Islam, living on the outskirts of the community, less exposed to the teachings and less integrated. Their behaviour during Tabūk was a test of their sincerity. The verse demonstrates that Allah’s judgment is based on truth and faithfulness, and that no one can escape punishment by simply hiding in the desert or offering flimsy excuses. It also serves as a reminder that the commands of Allah apply to all believers, urban or nomadic, and that those who claim faith while being untrue to it face severe consequences.",
  "91": "After condemning those who made false excuses, the verse establishes the categories of people who are genuinely exempt. The “weak” include the elderly and the physically frail. The “sick” are those with illnesses that prevent them from undertaking the arduous journey. “Those lacking the means” are the poor who cannot find mounts or provisions even if they desired to go. These groups bear no sin for staying behind. However, the verse adds a crucial condition: “as long as they are true to Allah and His Messenger.” Their inability must be genuine, and their hearts must be sincere. They should not be secretly pleased to be exempt, nor should they spread discouragement. They are considered among the “good-doers” (muḥsinīn) because their intention is pure and they would go if they could. The verse then closes with two divine names: All‑Forgiving and Most Merciful, indicating that any shortcoming in their effort that falls short of the ideal is covered by Allah’s immense mercy. This verse is a powerful example of the balance in Islamic law: obligations are matched to capacity, and sincerity of the heart is the ultimate criterion. It also provides comfort to those who are forced by legitimate circumstances to miss out on great acts of worship; their reward is secure if their hearts are with the believers.",
  "92": "This verse describes one of the most moving scenes of the Tabūk preparations. A group of sincere, poor believers approached the Prophet and asked for animals to ride so they could join the expedition. The Prophet, peace be upon him, had to turn them away because there were simply no mounts available. Their response was not relief but profound grief. They turned away “with eyes overflowing with tears,” weeping not from disappointment of a personal desire, but from the sorrow that they could not contribute to the cause of Allah. They had nothing to give and no way to go. This verse exonerates them completely from any blame. Their tears are a testimony to their deep faith. In the books of sīrah, these men are known as the “Weepers” (al‑Bakkā’ūn). They included people like ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal and others. The Prophet later managed to find mounts for some of them, but the verse immortalises their state of sincere longing. This teaches that the value of a believer’s intention can surpass the value of the action itself. Their inability did not diminish their rank; rather, their tears elevated them. It also shows the immense love the Companions had for jihad and their fear of missing out on the reward. For all believers, it sets a standard: when a good deed is desired but blocked by genuine incapacity, the tears of that sorrow are a sign of a living heart.",
  "93": "After excusing the sincere, the verse returns to the blameworthy. The blame is exclusively on the wealthy and able who came to the Prophet asking to be excused. They had the means—health, mounts, and provisions—but they chose to seek exemption. Their request was not based on necessity but on preference. “They preferred to stay behind with the helpless” repeats the earlier judgment, highlighting the shame of their choice. Then the verse states that “Allah has sealed their hearts so they do not realize.” This sealing is the result of their own persistent dodging of responsibility. Their repeated exposure to the truth, combined with their refusal to act on it, caused a spiritual insensitivity. They do not realize the gravity of their situation or the eternal loss they are incurring. The verse thus draws a clear line: capacity creates responsibility. Those who have means and still seek to avoid sacrifice are in a state of grave spiritual danger. It also warns that the habit of making excuses can harden the heart until it no longer feels the sting of its own failures.",
  "94": "The verse anticipates the moment when the victorious army returns to Medina. The hypocrites who stayed behind will rush to present their excuses, trying to justify their absence. The believers are instructed to reject these excuses outright. The command “Make no excuses, for we will not believe you” is a powerful refusal to engage with their lies. The reason is that Allah has already informed the believers of the true state of the hypocrites’ faith. Divine revelation has exposed their inner reality, so no human testimony can overturn that. However, the verse does not end with a complete closure. It adds that their future deeds will be observed by Allah and His Messenger. This is both a warning and an opportunity: they are being watched, and if they genuinely change, that will be known. But if they persist, they will be returned to the Knower of the seen and unseen on the Day of Judgment, and then they will be fully informed of what they used to do. The verse combines firmness with a reminder of ultimate accountability. It teaches that the community is not required to be naive; when Allah has clarified a matter, believers can act on that knowledge. Yet the final judgment remains with Allah, and the door of sincere repentance is not declared shut for individuals.",
  "95": "The hypocrites will resort to their usual tactic of swearing by Allah, hoping that their solemn oaths will persuade the believers to stop reproaching them and to leave them in peace. Their goal was to be left alone—to be socially accepted without having to account for their actions. The divine instruction is surprising: “So leave them alone.” This is not an acceptance of their excuses, but a form of punishment. It means to boycott them, to withdraw from engaging with them, to treat them as if they are irrelevant. The verse immediately explains why such a harsh measure is appropriate: “they are truly evil,” an abomination in character. Their place is Hell, and that is the fitting reward for their deeds. This response was more painful to the hypocrites than open confrontation, because it denied them the social validation they craved. The lesson is that there are times when the best course with insincere people is not argument but a dignified disengagement, leaving them to their own corruption and to the judgment of Allah.",
  "96": "This verse reinforces the point about the hypocrites’ oaths. Their swearing is a calculated effort to regain the believers’ favour. They want the believers to be pleased with them so they can enjoy the benefits of community membership. But the verse declares that human pleasure is irrelevant if Allah’s pleasure is absent. Even if every single believer were somehow satisfied with their excuses, Allah would never be pleased with the fāsiqūn, the rebellious who have left the path of obedience. This is a profound recalibration of priorities. The ultimate goal is not to please people but to please Allah. The hypocrites’ entire strategy was based on managing public perception, but they neglected the One whose opinion truly matters. For the sincere believers, this verse is a warning against seeking human approval at the expense of divine approval. It also liberates them from the need to win the favour of people whose hearts are corrupt, because that favour is worthless in the divine scales.",
  "97": "This verse offers a sociological observation about the Bedouins. Because they lived far from the centre of learning in Medina, their exposure to the Qur’an and the Prophet’s teachings was less frequent. Consequently, their disbelief and hypocrisy, when it existed, was “far worse” and more hardened. They were “less likely to know the limits revealed by Allah,” meaning the boundaries of what is lawful and unlawful, the obligations and prohibitions. This was due to the harsh desert environment, which often produced rough manners, and their distance from the community of knowledge. However, the verse does not excuse them; it simply explains the reason for the severity of their condition. It then reminds that Allah is All‑Knowing of their states and All‑Wise in His legislation and in differentiating between people based on their circumstances. This verse teaches the importance of being near to the sources of sacred knowledge. Isolation from the community and from regular exposure to revelation can lead to a coarsening of faith and a weakening of moral sensitivity. It also calls for understanding that people’s level of religious comprehension varies, and those with less knowledge may need more patience and teaching, though they remain accountable for what they have received.",
  "98": "The verse now exposes the deep malice of some Bedouins. When they spent anything in charity or for the expedition, they considered it a fine or a loss, not an investment for the Hereafter. They gave reluctantly, feeling they were being forced to pay. Worse, they awaited misfortunes to strike the believers—hoping for defeat, for disaster, for the mission to fail. Their hearts were so twisted that the success of Islam was a source of pain to them. The Qur’an responds with a powerful curse: “May ill-fortune befall them!” This is a prayer that the very misfortune they wish upon the believers should rebound upon them. It is a rare instance of a direct curse in the Qur’an, indicating the severity of their enmity. The verse concludes by stating that Allah is All‑Hearing of their words and All‑Knowing of their intentions. This is both a comfort to the believers—that the hidden malice is not hidden from Allah—and a warning to the hypocrites that their secret hopes are fully recorded. For the believers, it reinforces the idea that donations in the path of Allah should be given with a generous heart, expecting reward, not treating them as a financial loss. The attitude of the giver is a key indicator of faith.",
  "99": "The picture of the Bedouins is now balanced. Just as there were hard‑hearted hypocrites among them, there were also sincere believers. These nomads truly believed in Allah and the Last Day. Their spending was not seen as a loss but as qurubāt—a means of drawing nearer to Allah. They also deeply valued the prayers of the Messenger, peace be upon him. The Prophet would pray for those who brought charity, asking Allah to bless them. For these Bedouins, that supplication was a treasured gift, a spiritual connection that they sought. The verse assures them that their donations will indeed bring them closer to Allah. The word “certainly” emphasises the certainty of this promise. Their physical distance from Medina did not prevent their hearts from being close. The verse concludes by saying Allah will admit them into His mercy, and that He is All‑Forgiving and Most Merciful. This is a beautiful testament to the fact that true faith can flourish anywhere, even in the remote desert. It also highlights the importance of having the right intention in charity: to seek closeness to Allah, not to avoid criticism or show off. The prayers of the righteous are a blessing, and seeking them is a sign of humility and faith. This verse gives hope to all those who are physically far from centres of learning but whose hearts are attached to Allah and His Messenger.",
  "100": "This verse crowns the discussion of the different groups by mentioning the very best of the community. The “foremost” (al‑sābiqūn al‑awwalūn) are the earliest and most devoted among the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār—those who embraced Islam in Mecca before the Hijrah and those who welcomed them in Medina. This includes the participants of Badr and those who pledged allegiance under the tree. Their rank is the highest. The verse then extends the honour to “those who follow them in goodness,” meaning all subsequent generations of believers who follow the path of the earliest Companions in faith and righteous deeds, until the Day of Judgment. The greatest reward mentioned is not the Gardens but “Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him.” This mutual contentment is the pinnacle of spiritual attainment. Allah’s pleasure is the source of all bliss, and their pleasure is the result of receiving His boundless favour. The Gardens are then described as already prepared, a gift waiting for them. The eternity of the stay and the declaration that it is the ultimate triumph bring the passage to a majestic close. For the believers, this verse establishes the hierarchy of honour and motivates them to align themselves with the path of the early generations. It affirms that the true triumph is not in wealth or power but in earning Allah’s pleasure and entering His Paradise.",
  "101": "After praising the foremost believers in the previous verse, the sūrah returns to the reality of hidden opposition. The verse states plainly that hypocrisy was not confined to the city; it existed among the nomadic tribes around Medina and among the settled residents of the city itself. The phrase “they have mastered hypocrisy” translates the Arabic maradū ʿalā n-nifāq, meaning they became deeply entrenched, skilled, and persistent in it. Hypocrisy became their second nature. The verse then addresses the Prophet with a limitation of his knowledge: “They are not known to you; they are known to Us.” This is a profound reassurance. Despite the Prophet’s insight and the signs of hypocrisy that sometimes surfaced, there remained a segment of hypocrites whose true state was completely hidden from him. Allah alone knew them. This protected the Prophet from the burden of judging hearts and allowed him to deal with them based on their outward profession. The phrase “We will punish them twice in this world” has been interpreted in several ways by the Companions and early scholars. Some said it refers to the humiliation they suffered through exposure and the constant fear of being found out, combined with a painful illness or death. Others said the first punishment is the distress of seeing Islam’s victory and their own isolation, and the second is the punishment of the grave. Still others linked it to being forced to pay zakāh and participate in campaigns they hated. Regardless, the double punishment in this world is followed by a “tremendous punishment” in the Hereafter. This verse teaches the believers that unseen hypocrisy is a grave spiritual disease, but it also reassures them that ultimate judgment rests with the All‑Knowing. It warns that persistent insincerity brings suffering even in this life, not just the next.",
  "102": "This verse introduces a group distinct from the entrenched hypocrites. These were believers who had stayed behind from the Tabūk expedition but did not resort to lies and false excuses. Instead, they openly confessed their sin, acknowledging their failure without justification. The verse says they “mixed goodness with evil”—their earlier righteous deeds were tarnished by this serious lapse, but the foundation of faith was still present. Because of their honesty, the verse offers a powerful word of hope: “It is right to hope that Allah will turn to them in mercy.” The Arabic particle ʿasā used here, when coming from Allah, indicates a near certainty. Among those who fell into this category were individuals who tied themselves to the pillars of the mosque in Medina, vowing not to free themselves until the Prophet and Allah forgave them. One well‑known example is Abū Lubābah, who had earlier committed a different error and bound himself to a pillar until his repentance was accepted. Their confession was not a private whisper but a public acknowledgment, demonstrating true remorse. The verse closes with two divine names, All‑Forgiving and Most Merciful, to emphasise that the door of divine pardon is wide open for those who are truthful about their faults. This passage teaches that the worst state is not sin itself but the refusal to admit it. Honest confession, even after a major failure, can lead to complete forgiveness, while denial and excuses lead to the hardening of the heart.",
  "103": "After their confession, Allah instructs the Prophet to take ṣadaqah from their wealth. This charity serves two purposes: to purify them from the stain of their sin and to cleanse their wealth and souls. The word tuhirruhum indicates a cleansing of their spiritual state, and tuzakkīhim means to increase and bless them in goodness. The act of giving charity was a tangible proof of their repentance and a means of making amends. The verse then commands the Prophet to pray for them. His supplication was not a mere formality; it was a source of profound comfort (sakan) for their troubled hearts. Knowing that the Messenger of Allah was praying for their forgiveness brought them peace and relief. This incident shows the unique role of the Prophet as an intercessor and spiritual father to his community, and it underlines the importance of sincere charity accompanied by the prayers of the righteous. The verse ends by stating that Allah is All‑Hearing of their confessions and All‑Knowing of their intentions, so no sincerity goes unnoticed. In a broader sense, this established the principle that charity is a means of purification from sins, and the leader’s prayer for the community is a blessing. It also teaches that those who err can transform their mistake into an opportunity for good by giving generously and seeking the prayers of the pious.",
  "104": "This rhetorical question redirects the focus of those who repented—and indeed all believers—to the ultimate source of acceptance. While the Prophet was commanded to take their charity and pray for them, the verse reminds everyone that it is Allah alone who truly accepts repentance. The phrase “receives their charity” means He takes it with His right hand, as mentioned in a hadith, nurturing it until it grows like a mountain. The acceptance of charity is a divine act; the Prophet’s role is a means, but the actual acceptance is entirely in Allah’s hands. The verse uses two intensive names of Allah: at‑Tawwāb, the One who constantly turns in mercy to those who repent, and ar‑Raḥīm, the Most Merciful. This is meant to inspire hope in the hearts of sinners. No matter how grave the error, the door of return is open, and the Lord is eager to forgive. The rhetorical question also serves as a gentle rebuke to those who might focus too much on the external validation of their repentance through the Prophet’s prayer while forgetting the inner relationship with Allah. The verse teaches that repentance and charity must be directed sincerely to Allah, and that His acceptance is the only real guarantee of forgiveness.",
  "105": "At first glance, “Do as you will” seems like an open permission, but it is a powerful and stern warning. It follows the encouragement to repent and give charity, and it addresses those who might waver between sincerity and hypocrisy. The phrase means: act as you choose, but know that your actions are under constant surveillance. Allah observes everything, the Messenger is informed of the community’s true state through revelation, and the believers will witness the outward fruits of your deeds. This triple observation leaves no room for hidden misdeeds. The verse then points to the ultimate return to Allah, the Knower of the seen and the unseen, who will inform every soul of the full reality of its actions on the Day of Judgment. This is not a licence to sin; it is a threat wrapped in a command. It is similar to the statement, “Do whatever you want, for I know what you are doing.” The effect on the listener is meant to be profound introspection. For the believers, it instils a constant awareness of accountability. For the hypocrites, it warns that their pretence will eventually be exposed. The verse is a masterful psychological tool that employs the concept of surveillance—both divine and communal—to inspire righteous behaviour.",
  "106": "This verse introduces the case of the three famous Companions who remained behind from Tabūk without a valid excuse but did not lie about it. They were Kaʿb ibn Mālik, Murārah ibn ar‑Rabīʿ, and Hilāl ibn Umayyah. Unlike the hypocrites who offered false excuses and were dealt with firmly, and unlike those who confessed and gave charity, these three were placed in a state of suspense. The verse says they are “left for Allah’s decision,” meaning the Prophet received no revelation concerning their fate. The entire Muslim community was ordered to boycott them; no one was to speak to them. They were neither punished nor forgiven, and the uncertainty was agonising. The verse states that Allah may either punish them or turn to them in mercy, and He is All‑Knowing about their inner truth and All‑Wise in the way He is dealing with them. This period of suspension lasted for fifty days, and it served as a profound purification for their souls. The verse teaches that divine mercy is not to be taken for granted; even sincere but negligent believers can be subjected to severe spiritual trials to refine them. It also shows that Allah’s wisdom sometimes delays relief to deepen a servant’s repentance and reliance upon Him.",
  "107": "This verse exposes the story of Masjid aḍ‑Ḍirār, the Mosque of Harm. A group of hypocrites, led by Abū ʿĀmir ar‑Rāhib, a man who had fought against Islam and fled to the Byzantines, built a mosque near Qubā’. They claimed it was for prayer, especially for the sick and on rainy nights, and they asked the Prophet to come and pray there, thereby giving it his seal of approval. But the Qur’an reveals their four hidden objectives: to cause harm to the believers by drawing them away from the true mosque, to promote disbelief through secret meetings, to divide the community, and to serve as a safe house for the enemy, Abū ʿĀmir, who was plotting to return with a Byzantine army. The phrase “they will definitely swear” indicates their deceptive oaths that their intentions were purely good. But Allah bears witness that they are liars. The verse teaches the grave danger of religious institutions being used as fronts for subversive activities. It also warns against being deceived by fine‑sounding religious language when the underlying aim is to split the community. The intention behind any sacred work is what determines its value in the sight of Allah.",
  "108": "In this verse, the Prophet is categorically forbidden from praying in the Mosque of Harm. The command “Do not ever pray in it” serves to delegitimise the structure entirely. The Prophet’s prayer would have been seen as validation, so this prohibition was essential. The verse then contrasts it with the mosque “founded on righteousness from the first day.” According to many Companions, this refers to the Mosque of Qubā’, which the Prophet himself helped build when he first arrived in Medina during the Hijrah. Its very foundation was laid upon taqwā—God‑consciousness and sincerity. Others said it could refer to the Prophet’s Mosque itself. The phrase “in it are men who love to be purified” specifically praised the people of Qubā’, who were exceptionally meticulous in personal cleanliness, using water for purification. When the Prophet asked them what they did, they told him they used stones and then water, and he confirmed this was the source of the praise. Allah’s love for those who purify themselves encompasses both physical purity and spiritual purification from sins. The verse establishes that the sanctity of a mosque is not in its structure but in the sincerity of its founders and the purity of its congregation. It also highlights the high status of the Qubā’ Mosque and the virtue of cleanliness.",
  "109": "This verse presents a powerful parable to illustrate the contrast between the two mosques. The first building is founded on taqwā—the fear of Allah and the desire for His pleasure. This foundation is solid, rooted in the firmest ground, and it leads the builder upwards towards acceptance and Paradise. The second building is founded on the very edge of a cliff that is actively crumbling and giving way. The foundation is so unstable that it cannot support the structure, and inevitably the whole building collapses, plunging the builder into the depths of Hellfire. The imagery is terrifying: a person who thought he was constructing something beneficial discovers that his work is the cause of his destruction. The rhetorical question “Which is better?” leaves the answer obvious. The verse concludes by stating that Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people, meaning those who build their actions on deceit and hypocrisy are left to the consequences of their own choices. This parable applies not only to mosques but to all deeds. Any action, no matter how outwardly impressive, if built on insincerity, arrogance, or a desire to harm, is like that unstable cliff edge. It will collapse under the weight of divine scrutiny and take its builder to ruin.",
  "110": "The physical structure they built did not go away immediately; even after exposure, it remained a monument to their treachery. The verse says that this building “will never cease to fuel hypocrisy in their hearts.” It served as a constant source of doubt and insincerity, a reminder of their failed plot and their stubbornness in not repenting. The phrase “until their hearts are torn apart” is a graphic description of death or of the ultimate destruction of their inner selves. Some scholars say it means they will die as hypocrites, their hearts consumed by the very division they sought to sow. Others say it refers to the moment when the mosque itself was torn down by the order of the Prophet after his return from Tabūk. When they saw their work demolished, their hearts were shattered with grief and rage. The verse concludes with Allah being All‑Knowing of their hidden schemes and All‑Wise in the command to destroy their mosque and expose them. The lesson is that a sinful project, even if it stands physically, continues to corrode the souls of those behind it. The only cure is to demolish the wrong and turn to Allah in repentance.",
  "111": "After exposing the mosque of hypocrisy, the sūrah shifts to one of the most uplifting verses in the entire Qur’an. The commercial metaphor is used to describe the relationship between Allah and the believers. Allah, the Creator and Owner of everything, purchases from the believers their own lives and wealth—which He initially gave them—and in return grants them Paradise. This is an extraordinary act of divine generosity. The believers pay with their mortal, fleeting existence and receive eternal, perfect bliss. The transaction is carried out through jihad: they fight and are killed or kill, but in both cases the price is paid and the reward guaranteed. The verse then declares that this promise is a binding truth, recorded not only in the Qur’an but also in the earlier scriptures—the Torah and the Gospel. This emphasizes the unity of the divine message and the centrality of sacrifice for God’s cause across all dispensations. The rhetorical question, “Whose promise is truer than Allah’s?” is an unanswerable challenge. It is followed by a command to rejoice in this bargain. The believers should feel not regret but immense joy for having made the most profitable trade imaginable. The verse concludes by calling this “the ultimate triumph.” No worldly gain, no victory on any battlefield, compares to the success of securing Paradise through this divine transaction. For the Companions and for all believers, this verse transforms the sacrifices of life and wealth from a loss into the wisest investment.",
  "112": "This verse describes the believers who are party to the great transaction. The list of their qualities begins with repentance, indicating that a believer is not sinless but is one who constantly turns back to Allah. They are devoted to worship, a life of servitude. They praise Allah in all conditions, which some commentators link to saying alḥamdulillāh, but it encompasses a general attitude of gratitude and recognition of Allah’s perfections. The term as‑sā’iḥūn, translated here as “who fast,” literally means those who journey or travel. Many early scholars, including Ibn ʿAbbās, explained that it refers to those who fast, because fasting is a spiritual journey away from desires. Others said it refers to those who travel for jihad or in pursuit of knowledge. The next qualities relate to prayer: bowing and prostrating. Then the social dimension appears: they encourage good and forbid evil, and they observe the limits set by Allah, meaning they respect His boundaries in all aspects of life. The verse ends with a command to give good news to such believers. This good news is the Paradise of the previous verse. The qualities listed show that the faith is comprehensive, covering inward repentance, acts of worship, and social responsibility. It contrasts sharply with the hypocrisy described earlier.",
  "113": "This verse establishes a definitive boundary in the believers’ relationships with polytheists, particularly deceased relatives. The Prophet and the believers are forbidden from praying for the forgiveness of those who died upon shirk. The phrase “even if they were close relatives” underscores that the bond of faith supersedes the bond of blood when it comes to matters of the Hereafter. The condition “after it has become clear to the believers that they are bound for the Hellfire” means when a person dies in a state of clear disbelief, without repentance, their fate is sealed, and intercession for their forgiveness is no longer appropriate. This verse was revealed in relation to the Prophet’s desire to pray for his uncle Abū Ṭālib, who had protected him but did not embrace Islam before death. When the Prophet sought to ask forgiveness for him, this verse and others prohibited it. Some believers also wished to pray for their pagan parents. The verse teaches that while kindness and justice to living non‑Muslim relatives are commanded, seeking divine forgiveness after their death on shirk is a transgression of the limits set by Allah. This preserves the purity of the faith and emphasises the seriousness of dying in a state of disbelief.",
  "114": "An objection could arise: did not Abraham pray for his father’s forgiveness, and is he not a model for the believers? This verse clarifies the context. Abraham’s prayer was not an ongoing practice; it was based on a specific promise he had made to his father that he would ask Allah to forgive him, hoping his father might yet believe. But when it became absolutely clear to Abraham—whether through his father’s death on disbelief or through divine revelation—that his father was an unchangeable enemy of Allah, he immediately dissociated himself and stopped the prayer entirely. The verse then praises Abraham as “tender-hearted” (awwāh) and “forbearing” (ḥalīm). Awwāh is a term indicating deep compassion, frequent sighing out of fear and love of Allah, and heartfelt supplication. His forbearance is his patience in the face of his people’s opposition. This verse makes clear that Abraham is not a precedent for praying for polytheists after their death. His prayer was conditional, temporary, and abandoned once the truth was manifest. So the prohibition in the previous verse remains intact, and Abraham’s example in this specific matter is not to be followed, while his other noble qualities are to be emulated.",
  "115": "This verse articulates a fundamental principle of divine justice: Allah does not hold people accountable for a prohibition until it has been clearly communicated to them. A community that has received guidance is not declared astray or punished for a matter that was ambiguous or unknown to them. Only after the limits are made explicitly clear, through revelation, can those who violate them be considered deviant. This verse was revealed to comfort those believers who had previously prayed for their deceased polytheist relatives before the prohibition of verse 113 was sent down. They feared they had committed a grave sin. This verse assures them that since the prohibition had not yet been clarified, they would not be held accountable for their past actions. It also explains why Abraham’s earlier prayer was not held against him. The verse establishes that ignorance of a ruling is a valid excuse before the law is known, but once knowledge comes, obedience is obligatory. It also reminds that Allah has full knowledge of everything, so His timing in revealing laws is perfectly wise.",
  "116": "This verse reinforces the absolute sovereignty of Allah, which underlies all the commands and prohibitions in this passage. Everything in the heavens and the earth is under His dominion. He is the sole giver of life and death; no one can escape His decree. The verse then draws the practical conclusion: since Allah alone holds all power, besides Him humanity has no true guardian or helper. This demolishes any reliance on polytheist relatives, wealth, or human allies. The verse connects directly to the preceding prohibition of seeking forgiveness for polytheists and the call to complete loyalty to Allah. It teaches that all protection and aid come from the Creator, so to compromise faith for the sake of worldly ties is to abandon the only real source of help. For the believers, this is a source of strength and independence from the pressures of family and society.",
  "117": "This verse reflects on the Tabūk expedition, known as the “time of hardship” (ʿusrah). It was a moment of extreme difficulty due to heat, distance, and scarcity of provisions. The believers who marched out were the sincere Muhājirūn and Anṣār. The verse says that Allah turned in mercy to the Prophet first, then to the Emigrants and the Helpers. The mention of the Prophet, who is sinless, indicates Allah’s gracious acceptance of his efforts and his leadership during the hardship. For the believers, it acknowledges that “the hearts of a group of them had almost faltered.” Some of them experienced severe doubt and anxiety, wavering under the immense pressure. But they did not give in, and Allah accepted their repentance for any inner weakness. He then turned to them in mercy. The verse describes Allah as Ra’ūf (Ever Gracious) and Raḥīm (Most Merciful) specifically “to them,” showing a special divine care for that generation. This is a beautiful reassurance that even the greatest Companions were human, susceptible to moments of weakness, but divine mercy enveloped them because of their sincerity and ultimate perseverance.",
  "118": "The story of the three—Kaʿb ibn Mālik, Murārah ibn ar‑Rabīʿ, and Hilāl ibn Umayyah—is narrated in great detail in the authentic hadith. After being boycotted for fifty days, their psychological state became unbearable. The verse describes how “the earth, despite its vastness, seemed to close in on them.” This is a powerful image of claustrophobic guilt; they felt trapped, with no escape. Their souls were “torn in anguish,” a deep inner torment. In that state, they realised with absolute certainty that there is no refuge from Allah except in Allah Himself. No human being, no cave, no distance could save them from His judgment. So they turned entirely to Him in supplication and repentance. Then, at the end of this agonising trial, Allah turned to them in mercy. The phrase “so that they might repent” indicates that the trial itself was designed to guide them to a profound, transformative repentance. The verse seals their story with the names at‑Tawwāb and ar‑Raḥīm. Their enduring legacy in the Qur’an is a testament to the fact that even grave neglect of duty, when met with absolute truthfulness and sincere repentance, leads to divine forgiveness and eternal honour.",
  "119": "After the story of the three, whose salvation came through their truthfulness, the Qur’an addresses all believers with a concise, comprehensive command. “Be mindful of Allah” is the instruction to cultivate taqwā, a constant awareness of Allah that leads to obedience and avoidance of sin. The command “and be with the truthful” links taqwā directly to one’s social environment. It is not enough to have a personal intention of righteousness; one must keep the company of the ṣādiqīn—those who are truthful in their speech, sincere in their actions, and who do not waver in their commitment to the truth. The three were ultimately saved because they aligned themselves with truthfulness, refusing to lie as the hypocrites did. This verse teaches that character is deeply influenced by companionship. To remain steadfast in faith, a believer must associate with those whose words and deeds match their inner reality. The verse is a timeless call for integrity and wise selection of friends and community.",
  "120": "This verse addresses the obligation of supporting the Messenger and participating in the struggle. It states that it was not fitting for the residents of Medina and the surrounding Bedouins to stay behind while the Prophet marched out, or to consider their own lives more precious than his. The life of the Prophet was the most precious to the community, and his safety and mission were of paramount importance. The verse then offers a profound spiritual consolation: every hardship endured in the path of Allah is not wasted. Thirst, fatigue, and hunger—the common sufferings of a military campaign—are recorded as righteous deeds. Even the mere act of entering enemy territory in a way that frightens the disbelievers, or inflicting any loss upon them, is written as a good deed. Nothing is too small to be rewarded. “Surely Allah never discounts the reward of the good-doers” assures the believers that their sacrifices, no matter how seemingly insignificant, are fully preserved and magnified in the divine scales. This verse transforms the physical pains of struggle into acts of worship and sources of reward, motivating the believers to never hold back from supporting the cause of Allah and His Messenger.",
  "121": "Building upon the previous verse's promise that no hardship in Allah's cause goes unrewarded, this verse extends that assurance to every financial sacrifice and every physical movement undertaken for the sake of Allah. The phrase \"whenever they make a donation, small or large\" is profoundly inclusive. Nothing is too minor to be noticed. Whether a believer gives a mountain of gold or merely a handful of dates, it is recorded. This was a direct comfort to both the wealthy Companions who had given huge sums for the Tabūk expedition and the poor who had brought whatever meagre amount they could, such as the half a measure of dates that the hypocrites had mocked. Both donations, so vastly different in material terms, are equally \"written to their credit\" when given with sincerity. The verse then adds \"or cross a valley,\" referring to the actual physical marching through difficult terrain. Every step taken, every valley traversed, every mile covered in fatigue and heat is not just a military movement but a spiritual act being recorded by the angels. The phrase \"it is written to their credit\" implies a meticulous divine accounting, where nothing is lost. The purpose stated is \"so that Allah may grant them the best reward for what they used to do.\" The \"best reward\" means a recompense far exceeding the actual deed. A single coin given sincerely can be multiplied into a mountain of reward. This verse instils in the believer a sense that every moment of the struggle, and every expenditure, is an investment with guaranteed, multiplied returns. It demolishes the notion that mundane logistics of a campaign are separate from worship. For believers across time, this transforms daily acts of sacrifice—giving charity even when it is hard, commuting for a good cause, enduring tiredness in service of others—into deeds of immense spiritual worth. It encourages a life where one consciously dedicates even the smallest acts to Allah, knowing that He records and rewards them beyond imagination.",
  "122": "After the intense call to jihad and the rebuke of those who stayed behind, this verse establishes a crucial balance. It explains that not every believer needs to go out on every military expedition. The phrase \"it is not necessary for the believers to march forth all at once\" relieves the community from the burden of total mobilisation for every campaign. The early Muslim community was small, and if all had left, the city would have been defenceless and the sources of learning would have dried up. So the verse commands that from each tribe or group, only a party should go forth for jihad. The rest—those who remain—are to engage in a different but equally important form of struggle: \"to gain religious knowledge then enlighten their people when they return to them.\" This is the foundation of the Islamic duty of seeking knowledge and teaching it. The Arabic term li-yatafaqqahū fī d-dīn means to deeply understand the religion, to attain fiqh, which is profound comprehension that goes beyond surface information. Those who stay behind are not idle; they are students whose task is to learn the Qur'an and the teachings of the Prophet as they are being revealed, and to then educate the returning soldiers. The reason is \"so that they too may beware of evil.\" The warriors returning from battle need spiritual refreshment and guidance, and the community as a whole needs constant reminders to maintain taqwā. This verse establishes that knowledge is a form of jihad, and that a community must have a segment dedicated to scholarship and teaching. The early Muslims implemented this by sending delegations to the Prophet to learn and then return to their tribes as teachers. Later, this verse became the basis for the obligation of seeking religious knowledge as a communal duty (farḍ kifāyah). It ensures that the faith is preserved and transmitted, and that military strength is balanced with spiritual and intellectual strength. Without this balance, a community can become powerful in arms but weak in understanding, which leads to eventual ruin.",
  "123": "This verse gives a strategic command to the Muslim state. The directive is to fight those disbelievers who are geographically close and pose an immediate threat. The early Muslims were to prioritise the hostile tribes and forces in Arabia before turning to distant enemies. The phrase \"let them find firmness in you\" translates ghilẓah, which means toughness, severity, and unyielding resolve. The enemy should encounter not weakness or hesitation but a community that is firm in its principles and formidable in its defence. This firmness is not cruelty but a projection of strength that deters aggression. The verse immediately balances this instruction with a reminder that \"Allah is with those mindful of Him.\" The firmness shown must be within the bounds of taqwā, not a descent into barbarism or transgression. The believer fights firmly but justly, and the presence of Allah's support is tied to maintaining that God‑consciousness. This verse was particularly important in the context of the Byzantine frontier and the remaining hostile tribes in Arabia. It outlined a military posture that was proactive and strong, yet always anchored in moral limits. For today's believers, this verse is a call to be unapologetically firm in upholding truth and justice, not to be pushed around or bullied into compromising principles, but at the same time to remain deeply mindful of Allah in every action. The combination of firmness and taqwā is the hallmark of a balanced Muslim community.",
  "124": "The sūrah now returns to the behaviour of the hypocrites when the Qur'an was being revealed. A new revelation was a test. The hypocrites would turn to each other and ask with sarcasm, \"Which of you has this increased in faith?\" They meant the question as a joke, implying that the so‑called believers were just gullible, being moved by mere words. This mockery was a sign of their spiritual deadness. The verse immediately supplies the true answer: for the believers, the revelation does increase their faith. Hearing the words of Allah, with their proofs, warnings, and promises, strengthens their conviction and deepens their understanding. The increase of faith is a reality; imān is not static. It goes up with obedience and remembrance and decreases with sin and neglect. Furthermore, the believers \"rejoice\" in the revelation. The new sūrah brings them joy because it is guidance, mercy, and a connection to their Lord. The hypocrites cannot comprehend this joy. This verse teaches that the response to the Qur'an is a diagnostic of the heart. When a person hears a verse and feels boredom, irritation, or a desire to ridicule, that is a symptom of a serious spiritual sickness. When the heart is drawn closer, feels awe, and experiences an increase in eagerness to obey, that is the mark of faith. For the believers, it is a call to approach the Qur'an with an open, receptive heart, seeking the increase in faith that is promised to those who truly listen.",
  "125": "The same revelation that benefits the believers harms the hypocrites. The phrase \"sickness in their hearts\" refers to the disease of doubt, hypocrisy, and malice. For them, each new sūrah does not increase guidance but \"increased them only in wickedness upon their wickedness.\" The word rijs means filth, impurity, and spiritual defilement. The revelation exposes their secret thoughts, warns of punishment, and demands sacrifices they are unwilling to make. Instead of submitting, they react with more hatred, more mockery, and more plotting, layering fresh evil on top of their existing evil. The verse predicts their end: \"they die as disbelievers.\" Their persistent rejection and their refusal to let the Qur'an purify them lead to a state where they leave this world in a state of kufr. This is a terrifying warning that exposure to divine truth can either be a cure or a poison, depending on the heart's disposition. The same sun that softens wax hardens clay. If a person approaches the Qur'an with arrogance and a desire to find fault, it only hardens their heart further. This verse calls for deep self‑examination each time one reads or hears the Qur'an: is it increasing me in submission and love, or in resistance and resentment? The difference is the difference between life and spiritual death.",
  "126": "This verse points to the repeated tests and exposures that the hypocrites faced. Every year, perhaps through a military campaign, a scandal, a death, or the exposure of one of their own, they were confronted with the reality of their hypocrisy and the truth of Islam. \"Tried once or twice every year\" could refer to the various expeditions in which their true colours were shown, or to the moments when the Prophet confronted them with revelations exposing their secrets. Yet despite these repeated wake‑up calls, they failed to repent and failed to take heed. The phrase \"they neither repent nor do they learn a lesson\" indicates a complete spiritual numbness. This is a universal pattern: a person who is on a wrong path will often receive multiple warning signs—a sudden difficulty, a friend's advice, a sermon that stings, a near‑miss with disaster. These are opportunities from Allah to turn back. The tragic condition is when these tests come and go and the person does not change, because the heart has become sealed. The verse is a call to be alert to the tests and reminders in one's own life, and to treat them as urgent signals for reflection and repentance before the heart becomes incapable of responding.",
  "127": "The scene described is vivid. When a sūrah is revealed in the Prophet's presence, and the believers listen with rapt attention, the hypocrites are uncomfortable. They glance nervously at each other, checking if any believers are observing their reactions. Their concern is not with the divine message but with human observation. \"Is anyone watching you?\" reveals their paranoia. If they sense they are not being watched, they slip away quietly, unable to bear the weight of the revelation. The verse then gives the spiritual reason: \"Allah has turned their hearts away.\" Their hearts are not merely misguided; they have been actively diverted from the truth by Allah as a punishment for their own choices. The phrase \"because they are a people who do not comprehend\" indicates the root cause: their refusal to use their intellect in the way Allah intended, to reflect and understand, has led to a divine seal. This verse teaches that when a person's primary concern becomes the judgment of people rather than the judgment of Allah, they lose the ability to benefit from revelation. It also shows that avoiding the company of the righteous and the settings where the Qur'an is recited is a sign of a diseased heart. The believer should be eager to be in such gatherings, not looking for an exit.",
  "128": "After all the stern commands, exposures, and warnings, the sūrah draws to a close with a tender description of the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him. This verse is placed here as a soft landing after the difficult journey of Sūrah At‑Tawbah. The address is to all humanity, or specifically to the Quraysh, and by extension to the entire Ummah. \"A messenger from among yourselves\" highlights his humanity and his connection to the people. He is not an angel or a stranger; he shares their lineage, their language, and their daily struggles. This makes his example imitable and his compassion relatable. The verse then lists three of his most beautiful qualities. First, he is deeply \"concerned by your suffering.\" The word ʿazīz means severe or hard; whatever causes the believers hardship is heavy upon him. He feels their pain and is distressed by the obstacles they face. Second, he is \"anxious for your well-being.\" The word ḥarīṣ indicates a burning desire; he is extremely eager for their guidance and salvation. He wants them to enter Paradise and escape the Fire, and his entire mission is driven by this urgency. Third, he is \"gracious and merciful\" specifically to the believers. Ra’ūf and Raḥīm are two names of Allah, here applied to the Prophet in a derived sense. He is exceptionally gentle, kind, and forgiving with those who follow him. This description was meant to soften the hearts of the believers after the intensity of the surah and to remind the hypocrites and polytheists of the treasure they were rejecting. The verse calls every believer to love the Prophet with a deep love based on these qualities, and to strive to embody a fraction of that concern, eagerness, and mercy towards others.",
  "129": "The sūrah that began with a declaration of dissociation from the treaty‑breakers ends with a declaration of ultimate reliance. After the beautiful description of the Prophet’s character, Allah instructs him on what to say if the people still turn away from his message. Their rejection is painful, but the Prophet is not left in despair. The formula given is a fortress for the soul. “Allah is sufficient for me” is ḥasbiya Allāh, a statement of complete contentment and dependence. Allah is enough; no human support, no worldly power is needed when He is on one’s side. “There is no god worthy of worship except Him” is the foundation of tawḥīd, reminding the Prophet that his mission is to affirm this truth regardless of the response. “In Him I put my trust” is tawakkul, the active entrusting of all affairs to Allah after doing one’s best. The verse concludes with “He is the Lord of the Mighty Throne,” emphasising Allah’s supreme dominion over the entire universe. The Throne is the greatest of all created things, and its Lord is the One who controls all. This is a source of immense comfort: the rejection of a few tribes is nothing before the Lord of the Mighty Throne. This verse was among the last to be revealed in this sūrah, and it served as a powerful reminder to the Prophet and to all believers that when facing opposition, the ultimate response is to turn to Allah, reaffirm one’s faith, and place absolute trust in the Lord of all creation. It is the perfect seal for a sūrah that dealt with enmity, hypocrisy, and the struggle for the soul of the community, bringing the focus back to the unshakeable bedrock of reliance on Allah alone."
};
